_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:582:There is no mechanical acceptance certificate attached. The important owner-visible blockers are OPEN-3, OPEN-14, OPEN-16, OPEN-19 and OPEN-22; several are specification gaps rather than language-spelling conflicts. This sheet refuses to resolve them by changing approved `as`, `if`, `match`, `#[…]`, or path spellings.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:594:| `newtype`, `.0` unwrap, builtin unwrap | [OD54](../docs/decisions.md#od54), [OD155](../docs/decisions.md#od155) | named structs / match as applicable |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:617:Also refuse exceptions/unwinding, capturing closures, trait objects/hidden dynamic dispatch, source macros/derive, derived equality, default and variadic functions, shadowing, implicit conversions and truthiness under the [binding refusal list](../docs/PHILOSOPHY.md#what-concrete-refuses) and [P16–P17](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-refusals). Diagnostics should point to explicit match/error arms, named functions/context, static dispatch, reviewed generated source, written arguments and canonical numeric conversions as applicable. General comptime and broad `#[cfg]` remain refused pending the stated workload/proof/audit reopening conditions; they are not permanent refusals. `ptr::cmp` is refused by [OD37](../docs/decisions.md#od37) (use the sign of ptr::diff), and reference-to-reference casts by OD103. No new refusal is decided here.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:627:1. **OPEN-1 — Source trivia/byte policy (slice 0).** Recommend valid UTF-8, space/tab/LF/CR whitespace, `//` to LF, CR or EOF, no block comments/BOM/shebang/raw NUL. Alternative: allow UTF-8 BOM and nested block comments. Reason: a small explicit byte language matches SYN-5/6 without inheriting host lexer behavior. CRLF leaves LF as whitespace.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:631:5. **OPEN-5 — Numeric token details (2/26; leading-zero decision already binds 0).** Recommend lowercase base prefixes, no separators; retain typed integer suffixes to preserve the approved Q6.2 example, with exact suffix grammar still for approval, digits-dot-digits floats without exponents; reject malformed adjacent numeric text as one diagnostic. Alternative: retain uppercase prefixes and add explicit separators/exponent forms. Reason: do not silently contradict `200u8` in the approved field example; freeze the accepted token language independently of lexer accidents; do not reopen decided decimal-leading-zero refusal.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:637:11. **OPEN-11 — Imports (17).** Recommend `import a.b.{x, y as z};`, nonempty list, mandatory commas, optional trailing comma after factoring; wildcard already refused by [MOD-2](../legacy/docs/spec/modules.md#MOD-2), no whole-module alias or alternate missing-dot form. Alternative: `import a.b {x, y as z};`. Reason: old parser accepts both final-dot styles, so the owner should choose one canonical form.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:642:16. **OPEN-16 — Blocks and LL(1) statement/value modes (3/10/20).** Recommend retain decided statement/value behavior, explicit function returns, no standalone block primary, and derive one common-prefix block grammar with delayed control-node classification as described in section 4. Alternative: require explicit grouping of tail if/match or a tail marker, **only after checking and explicitly revising any decided acceptance it changes**. Reason: a position-blind grammar with both `{stmt}` and optional expr is not LL(1); the independent grammar must settle this rather than omit value alternatives. Approval must include a mechanical check, not just the algorithm description.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:643:17. **OPEN-17 — Exact kept pattern/arm surface (9/10/16).** Recommend qualified enum patterns, `_` catch-all, integer/bool/char literals, OR patterns, named field renaming, whole-payload `{..}` and a mandatory comma after every arm. Keep irrefutable enum lets, mut applying to every binder, explicit field handling and the deferred status of nested patterns. Alternative: comma only after non-block arms, subject to FIRST/FOLLOW proof. Clarify OD25’s “bare” fieldless wording against the chapter’s qualification rule; explicitly disposition generic `E::<T>::V` and module-qualified let paths. Do not treat the frozen parser as authority for a new accepted form.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:645:19. **OPEN-19 — Borrow-bound delimiter and defer target (13–21).** Recommend place-only borrow source, top-level no-cast range_bound with parenthesized casts, else only for range form, no-else requires evidence; defer accepts a syntactic direct/associated/method call (indirect call when slice 20 admits it), checked Unit result, no arbitrary expression/block. Alternative: require parentheses around both borrow bounds and defer call arguments at a dedicated defer form. Reason: preserve Q4.7's as delimiter and Unit-only deferred calls while eliminating FIRST/FOLLOW ambiguity. Ordinary borrow syntax remains decided.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:648:22. **OPEN-22 — Brand/package/function-contract surface (19/20).** Recommend the explicit brand/exists/pack/unpack proposals in section 7 and named binder contracts on fn types, reusing ordinary explicit context parameters for function values. Alternative: scoped library eliminators plus named contract aliases. Reason: Core Q1 approves these mechanisms, while R-0601 explicitly still owns a binding design; surface syntax cannot be recovered from ANF constructors. Approve each binder, packaging expression, escape and loop-carried existential rule separately.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:651:25. **OPEN-25 — Two historical refusals needing located authority.** Recommend ratify refusal of value while…else and postfix member ->; keep the function result arrow. Alternative: consider each separately as a new proposal. Postfix ? is already permanently refused and is excluded from this question. Reason: historical removal alone must not be assigned an invented deciding OD.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:670:| 12 | Receivers | Agree | Only self/&self/&mut self; no self:T | Agree shorthand only | Keep shorthand as canonical receiver; typed self cannot add a second Destroy protocol. Separate proposal may remove dot calls. |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:672:| 14 | Types/generics | Agree | Agree | Kind-separated caps; type/brand before cap | Keep kind-neutral argument candidate, but explicitly reject cap names as ordinary types; ordering already constrained by OD88. |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:677:| 19 | Borrows/defer | Agree | Agree | Agree | Keep restriction proposal; Q4.7 clause combination remains explicit question. |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:687:Review record: v2 reconciles the final Codex (6 findings), Kimi (18 findings) and agy (10 findings plus citation checklist) sections. agy did not read the repository; every assertion is therefore checked here rather than treated as verified by that reviewer. See GRAMMAR_CHANGES.md for individual outcomes. Recommendations remain unapproved; the owner alone decides revisions. The reviewers have not reviewed this merged v2. No compiler, legacy gate or parser was run; there is no mechanical LL(1) certificate. Only this review artifact is requested for approval; it changes no language rule or implementation.
docs/decisions.md:11:The chapters state these decided rules now (owner revision, 2026-11-03), before being rewritten as Lean, the only normative source of the language
docs/decisions.md:13:the earlier behaviour, the answer below governs, and the code follows as its new-compiler slice lands.
docs/decisions.md:46:- `legacy/` kept until the new compiler implements a full real program such as RocksDB or ethrex ([row](#decided-project-restart)).
docs/decisions.md:78:| TRUST-8/9/10 | Tokens model resources, effects appear as token parameters, and there is no separate effect system. | Authority introduction and rules 1–2, 8, 11 | Fixed in prose 2026-11-03. |
docs/decisions.md:85:| MEM-5/7, TYPE-10, OWN-15 | `free(h)` releases storage and returns its payload; a Copy result can disappear as part of consumption. | OD59; clarified OD140 | Fixed in prose 2026-11-03. Allocator identities, dependency discipline and complete failure contracts remain [undecided: see the target Core proposal](#core-allocator-identities). |
docs/decisions.md:90:| TRAIT-D1 rationale | Impl target lookup includes a sibling submodule’s declarations as an implicit fallback. | Explicit-child decision, completed OD43 | Fixed in prose 2026-11-03. |
docs/decisions.md:133:  mutability and permitted positions, undecided on 2026-11-03, are provisionally decided by rows Q3.1–Q3.6 and Q4.7 ([rows](#decided-2026-11-05-core)); opening never yields an element reference.
docs/decisions.md:173:The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
docs/decisions.md:175:**The Core rows are a provisional approval (stage A2a).** They bind the design without freezing it. Stage A2b formalizes them in a proposed `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` and proves the four design theorems; any row the formalization changes returns to the owner; stage A2i integrates the parts (2026-11-06); the owner freezes Core at A2c (row Q9.19 as revised). Until a rule is written in Lean, the answer below governs over any chapter text that differs.
docs/decisions.md:181:- **TRAIT-1@1** (row Q2.8): two impls with equal heads may coexist only when Copy/Destroy disjointness (OD28) makes their domains disjoint, as in `impl<T: Copy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>` beside `impl<T: Destroy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>`. No other coherence or orphan rule changes, and no disjunctive bound syntax is added.
docs/decisions.md:182:- **The generated-chapter requirement** (rows Q9.15, P3): Lean is the specification, in one main normative file with doc comments and examples; no prose is generated from it. This confirms the [2026-11-04 decision](#decided-project-lean-is-the-spec). The "one main normative file" wording is revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): one authoritative specification library in cohesive modules, with `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` as its public import.
docs/decisions.md:184:- **The text-only slice of R4 F4.3** (row Q4.7) is replaced before it was recorded: one scoped form opens every view, `borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]`. Without `else` an invalid range traps, like indexing; with `else` it is handled; a proven invalid range without `else` is refused (OD118). Arrays need `a ≤ b ≤ len`; text also needs UTF-8 scalar boundaries at both byte offsets.
docs/decisions.md:185:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped.
docs/decisions.md:230:- **Q4.6** What are dynamic string views? — Byte offsets; `byte_at` traps out of range; subviews only through the scoped borrow form of Q4.7 (`borrow t from a until b as s { … } [else { … }]`). *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): byte_at needs evidence of an in-range offset.*
docs/decisions.md:231:- **Q4.7** How are scoped views and range subviews introduced? — (a) borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]; no else = trap (like indexing); proven invalid range without else refused (OD118). *Revises or adds: Replaces R4 F4.3 text-only mandatory-else form; extends Q3.1/Q3.2 with subview formation; preserves OD36/OD118.* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the form without `else` needs evidence of a valid range; a provably out-of-range view index is covered by the same rule.*
docs/decisions.md:237:- **Q6.2** Is local constant propagation mandatory? — Fixed table of statically known values: literals, immutable lets, module constants, checked ops, six named wrapping/saturating ops, lossless `as`. *Revised 2026-11-05: facts propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring ([revision](#decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields)).* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): known values remain facts; refusal widens to unproven safety.*
docs/decisions.md:243:- **Q7.1** How are target grant schemas and multiplicities specified? — One target, linux-x86_64-hosted (Linux ≥ 5.6), with a fixed grant table; main asks for grants as parameters.
docs/decisions.md:252:- **Q7.10** Do you approve the linux-x86_64-hosted grant table? — Approve R4 F6's complete grant table below as part of this row: every launch configuration, operation signature, permission, narrowing rule and ending contract. Standard-stream operations require Console (Q7.11), not Device; where the R4 F6 table says with(Device) for StdIn/StdOut/StdErr, read with(Console). A missing configured grant refuses startup with exit 125; a trap exits 134. *Revises or adds: New target contract: owner decision.* *Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the stack target contract (adequate stack excludes exhaustion, launch refusal 125 when the reservation is smaller than the bound; then a defined outcome for inadequate stack) is a future target-contract obligation, not a current guarantee.*
docs/decisions.md:255:- **Q8.2** What exact source subset is admitted? — A1 minimal `u8`/linear-struct component subset. <a id="q8-2-revision"></a>Confirmed 2026-11-06 by [vertical slices](#decided-project-vertical-slices-2026-11-06): this is slice 1; its defined trap is an explicit `abort()`, with the refused `200 + 56` as its known-violation control; integers are complete at slice 2.
docs/decisions.md:257:- **Q8.4** Does the first artifact qualify as a complete executable? — Component plus bound launcher first.
docs/decisions.md:265:- **Q9.5** Are heap opening and borrowing separate user mechanisms? — One surface borrow; distinct typed Core cases.
docs/decisions.md:286:- **P4** Duplicate enum variants or methods (bug 313) — Refuse duplicate variant names in one enum and duplicate method names in one impl, with a new rule and diagnostic, the same as duplicate fields.
docs/decisions.md:288:- **P6** Retention manifest gates deleting legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv at the root gives every legacy asset (corpus, regressions, examples, std, bug ledger, site, docs, research) a destination and deletion criterion. Revised 2026-11-06 ([restart row](#decided-project-restart)): nothing is deleted at B3; legacy/, the old pipeline included, is deleted way later (LD: once the new compiler implements a full real program such as RocksDB, ethrex or similar), when RETENTION.tsv has no live entry, a final ideas sweep has dispositioned every idea and the owner explicitly approves.
docs/decisions.md:290:- **P8** Server services under scripts/ — Keep only landing services: train, review, hook, landing bot. The status page, work queue, fuzz farm, perf tracking and nightly campaign all stop and are recorded as archived in RETENTION.tsv; each returns only with a roadmap task.
docs/decisions.md:293:- **P11** Std bugs and rulings are carried — Add a 'std' class: 12 std bugs move with std as contracts plus regressions. The 112 language rulings (26 still open) keep their ruling and become conformance examples.
docs/decisions.md:295:- **P13** concrete-ci-2 as the only CI host — CI runs on concrete-ci-2 (self-hosted) and on GitHub-hosted runners as a regular second pool, not only a fallback. Jobs that need ci-2 caches run there; the oracle is fetched as a release asset verified by digest; a check neither pool can run is incomplete and blocks landing. Self-hosted runners never run pull_request jobs.
docs/decisions.md:297:- **P15** New roadmap: retiring obligations — Accept the new single-file ROADMAP.md (wip/new-roadmap, reconciled: prepare → rehearse → move, assurance wording per Q8.1, Phase E per P18, decides nothing itself): 79 tasks kept as rows, 345 folded into area tasks with their obligations, 109 deferred with triggers, 7 retired, 15 done.
docs/decisions.md:298:- **P16** Status changes in the refusal list — Accept the new statuses in PHILOSOPHY.md. Now Permanent: operator overloading (OD26), capability hiding (authority rule 5), derived equality, mutable global state (OD8), umbrella capabilities and aliases, old/quantifiers in contracts (OD117), lexical shadowing (OD67), and ghost code that affects execution. Ghost code itself is admitted as decided (OD81). Foreign function values reopen when typed extern fn types are admitted (OD4); concurrency reopens on R-0399's trigger.
docs/decisions.md:303:- **P20** One home for binding decisions — Move the still-binding roadmap decisions (new compiler, retention contract, authority, allocation, Lean as the spec) into docs/decisions.md beside your language answers. The dated log becomes history; ROADMAP.md links to decisions.md and restates nothing.
docs/decisions.md:304:- **P21** Retention records outlive legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv, ISSUES.tsv, required-checks.txt, scripts/oracle/ and docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/ (with task_disposition.tsv) are part of the day-one root, outside legacy/, so they survive deleting it. *Revised 2026-11-06 (owner, verbatim: "do the whole movement"): of docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/, the files that are decision sources or disposition records stay at the root (task_disposition.tsv, the decision log ROADMAP_DECISIONS.md, the task register ROADMAP_TASKS.md, ROADMAP_RECONCILIATION.md, ROADMAP_RESTRUCTURE.md with its table, and the archive README); the old control plane ROADMAP.md, ROADMAP_BACKLOG.md, ROADMAP_DIRECTION.md and WORK_QUEUE.md were duplicates of their frozen legacy/ copies apart from the move's mechanical rewrites, and nothing cites them as a decision source, so their root copies were removed and links point at the legacy/ copies, which stay until the legacy deletion point. The other docs/archive/ records (the site takedown record, the old bug ledger's disposition, the landing audit and the dated decision archives) stay at the root.*
