legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:483:landed; the owner's new answer governs. A rule already on main stays as it is until the owner
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:496:| OD2 | <a id="od2"></a>`&mut` arguments: an implicit reborrow; overlapping exclusive arguments refused; a deferred call keeps its loan until it runs; one defined order for cleanup, loan end and write-back | D1 and §4.5; the reborrow implemented in landing train 6 (REF-10); the defer and exit-order rules not yet | exit 2i (G9, B1, B2), so every deliverable; R-0496 (Core reborrow form) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: (1) passing a `&mut` on is plain lending, each call's use ending when it returns, with no reborrow concept or syntax; (2) a call is refused when an exclusive argument overlaps any other argument (`h(r, r)`, `h(&mut x, &x)`, `h(&mut a, &mut a.f)` refused); as tightened by OD15 (a) the same day, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, so even `h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused; (3) `&mut` refers to the place, a write through it is the write to that place at once, and copy-in/copy-out is not part of the language (only an optimization proven equivalent); (4) `defer` keeps OWN-12 and a borrow passed to a deferred call stays live until the call runs; (5) one exit sequence on every non-abort exit: the exit value, then the scope's deferred calls (last registered first, innermost scope first), then the scope's loans end, then the remaining locals are destroyed; an abort runs none of it **Canonical current root-based exclusivity rule: REF-11 and OD15(a); this historical overlap formulation is a reference to that rule.** |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:500:| OD6 | <a id="od6"></a>Traits are coherent: one impl per (trait, type), the orphan rule, an ambiguous method call must be qualified, resolution by typed identity | D7; implemented by bug 186's repair (TRAIT-1 – TRAIT-9) | bug 186's repair (exit 0b), so every deliverable; R-0548, R-0549; V5 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: traits are coherent: one impl per (trait, type), the orphan rule, an ambiguous method call must be qualified, resolution by typed identity. Completed 2026-11-02: qualified as `Trait::method(&x, ...)` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)) **Canonical current qualification rule: this OD6 as completed by the audit below; the audit supplies syntax, not a second rule.** |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:502:| OD8 | <a id="od8"></a>Global state: immutable globals everywhere; mutable state only as declared cells in unsafe-marked modules, reached through capabilities; no `static mut` | D8; pending (TRUST-6, R-0509) | R-0509, R-0496; V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: Austral's capability model, extended. (1) Constants only: no mutable global state of any kind (no `static mut`, no cells); a constant is truly immutable, has no interior mutability and never contains a capability, token or handle. (2) Global resources are linear tokens minted at startup: `main` receives a root resource value (in the spirit of Austral's `RootCapability`) and splits it; hardware and runtime singletons (allocator arena, UART and MMIO, panic buffer, errno, C libraries with internal state) are produced or taken only by `trusted fn` wrappers returning or taking linear tokens, and a gate lists those wrappers. (3) Tokens are ordinary values: every effect shows as a token parameter, under the existing loan and exclusivity rules (OD1, OD2, OD15), with no separate effect system. (4) Interrupt handlers, FFI callbacks and future threads get only the tokens explicitly given to them, by installation, transfer or a lock granting a borrow. (5) No life before `main`, no cleanup protocol for global state, and abort runs nothing (OD10). (6) An implicit-context mechanism, if ergonomics later demands one, is a new owner decision, never a hidden global. The cell variant was rejected after the Codex and Grok reviews (interrupts, FFI re-entrancy, linear cell contents, effect polymorphism, concurrency). Now TRUST-8 to TRUST-10 (pending, R-0557) Revised 2026-11-01 – 11-02: items 2 and 3 are superseded by the authority decision (grants handed to `main`; `with(...)` plus resource values); item 5 stands ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:503:| OD9 | <a id="od9"></a>Modules and interfaces: one file per module, `pub` as the interface, interface artifacts generated by the compiler, no handwritten interface files | D9; pending (TIGHTENING_DECISIONS §5, step 6) | R-0492 and R-0497 (interface data); separate compilation in V6; OD20 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: one file per module with `pub` as the interface; interface artifacts generated by the compiler, no handwritten interface files; a gate diffs each module's generated interface between commits so any public-API change shows in review (R-0556) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:504:| OD10 | <a id="od10"></a>Arithmetic: checked by default, named wrapping and saturating operations, shifts with a checked count; an abort runs no deferred call | T-ARITH, recorded as settled (today's behaviour), with §4.5's abort rule | exits 2h and 2j, so V1a, V1b and V1c (checked `+ - *` and their traps); R-0493 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: integer arithmetic is checked by default; wrapping and saturating arithmetic are named operations; the shift count is checked; an abort runs no deferred call |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:505:| OD11 | <a id="od11"></a>Casts: `as` only when lossless by value domain; every lossy conversion is a named function | T-CAST: ARITHMETIC_POLICY §8 (2026-10-21) and §4.5's value-domain rule; not implemented | exit 2k; the V1 increment that admits casts | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: `as` only when lossless by value domain; every lossy conversion is a named function |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:509:| OD15 | <a id="od15"></a>G10: whole-owner freezing under a shared borrow stays. G19: no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity, and the operational loan rules are defined now | T-G10 (keep the freeze) and T-G19 (the policy sentence) | exit 2i (the G10 decision record), so every deliverable; R-0496, R-0505 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner. (a) Whole-owner freezing: a live borrow of any part of a value freezes the whole owner, and loans are keyed by the root binding (REF-11 as in the current compiler); this tightens OD2's part 2: at a call, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, even disjoint fields (`h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused, as in the current compiler); OD1's "keyed by the root variable" stands. (b) The strict option: the language guarantees `&mut` exclusivity, which the checker enforces (OD2, OD15a), and no compiler pass or optimization may assume or exploit it until the checker's guarantee is proven in Lean (R-0499 with the Core semantics R-0496); until then the operational loan rules are defined now, G19 included: a pointer or alias derived from a loan is valid only while that loan lives. Now REF-11@3 (partial, bug 319, which OD23 adds) and REF-14 (pending, R-0496) **Canonical current root-based exclusivity subject: REF-11 (including OD23’s re-lent case); OD2 and OD23 refer here rather than defining separate variants.** |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:510:| OD16 | <a id="od16"></a>A struct literal in an `if`, `while` or `match` head is refused unless parenthesized | not in the record. **Provisional (bug 212):** the current compiler reads an uppercase name followed by `{` in an `if`, `while` or `match` head as the start of a struct literal, refuses `if n > LIMIT { … }` with an E0001 that suggests parentheses, and states that case rule in `grammar/concrete.ebnf` and SYN-1; provisional until the owner answers | V1c (V1's surface grammar has conditionals and loops); R-0500 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: refused unless parenthesized, with no exceptions; the uniform rule replaces the uppercase-name heuristic (bug 212's provisional behaviour). Now SYN-4 (pending, bug 318); whether a literal inside a call's argument list in a head counts as parenthesized is OD34 |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:513:| OD19 | <a id="od19"></a>Outside the language list: the scope of [PROVABLE_V1](../verification/PROVABLE_V1.md), that is, whether discarded statements, guards and ranges are admitted | open; it holds the model-coverage branch (`wip/model-coverage-3`) | that branch's landing; exit 2c's model-diff coverage | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: the provable subset admits discarded statements, guards and ranges, each entering as its Core semantics lands (this unblocks `wip/model-coverage-3`) **Revised 2026-11-03: the guards and ranges fragments were superseded by OD55 and OD56 (2026-10-31), which removed those forms; discarded statements remain subject to the decided discard rules.** |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:515:| OD21 | <a id="od21"></a>Representation rights of a parent over its child module (added 2026-10-25, from bug 262): when a parent module imports its child's newtype or struct, every stage lets the parent construct the newtype and read and write the struct's private fields, because Check counts the children's declarations among the module's own for construction and field rights; Resolve also lets a parent name a child's newtype as a symbol without an import. Two options: enforce TYPE-11 as written (a newtype or private field is constructed or accessed only inside the module that declares it, so a child is another module), or amend TYPE-11 to grant a parent these rights over the declarations it imports from its children | the behaviour dates from construction rights slice 1 (2026-07-18); it contradicts TYPE-11@1's wording ("outside the module that declares it"); bug 262's record names `tests/programs/module_qualified_impl` as a program that applying TYPE-11 strictly would refuse; the wider impact is not yet measured (no `scripts/tools/rule_impact.sh` scan is recorded); bug 262's record (branch `wip/newtype-ctor-stage`) leaves the question to R-0011 and makes no recommendation | R-0011; the construction-rights fixtures (`module_qualified_impl` and bug 262's `error_262_*` and `regress_262_*` fixtures); bug 262's follow-up (whether Resolve binds a child's newtype in its parent) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: only the declaring module; a newtype is constructed, and a private field read or written, only inside the module that declares it, and a parent module gets no rights over its child (TYPE-11 enforced as written) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:516:| OD22 | <a id="od22"></a>Trust on an `impl` block (added 2026-10-25, from bug 189). Bug 189's repair made the parser refuse `trusted impl` (E0001) and moved trust onto each method (`trusted fn`, 175 methods marked), while TRUST-5@1 in [the specification](trusted.md#TRUST-5) still says a `trusted fn` or `trusted impl` may perform raw-pointer operations. Two options: keep the refusal and amend TRUST-5 to drop `trusted impl`, or restore `trusted impl` as TRUST-5@1 states it and revert bug 189's refusal | **Provisional (bug 189):** the current compiler refuses `trusted impl` and trusts only methods declared `trusted fn`; the specification text is unchanged, so the current compiler and TRUST-5@1 disagree; bug 189's record cites the 2026-10-23 rule that a marker never confers trust (D10, itself OD7, proposed); provisional until the owner answers | bug 189's repair (exit 0b) and its pins (`error_189_trusted_impl_refused.con`, `check_trusted_method.sh`); TRUST-5; R-0509's trusted-base inventory; OD7 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: trust is per method, `trusted fn` only; `trusted impl` stays refused, so bug 189's provisional repair is final |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:522:| OD28 | <a id="od28"></a>TRAIT-10: a `Destroy` bound is satisfied only by an impl (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | every Copy type and every array of destroyable elements counts as `Destroy`; bug 324 | exit 2q; R-0499 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): a `Destroy` bound is satisfied only by an impl; Copy types are not `Destroy` (discard them with `discard`); arrays get one written impl, or one language impl that is its own spec entry. Now TRAIT-10@2 (partial, bug 324); which array form is OD35 |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:525:| OD31 | <a id="od31"></a>ARITH-2: no `Int` fallback for an unconstrained literal (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | an unconstrained integer literal is `Int` and a float literal `Float64`; bug 327 | exit 2q; exit 2j | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): an integer literal takes its type from its context, as today; the fallback to `Int` where nothing is expected is removed, so an unconstrained literal is refused; suffixes are not required; float literals get an explicit rule under the same principle. Now ARITH-2@2 (partial, bug 327) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:527:| OD33 | <a id="od33"></a>Inline submodules under OD9 (added 2026-10-29, from recording OD9): OD9's answer is one file per module, and today the language also accepts an inline submodule (`mod m { ... }` inside its parent's file, used by most specification examples and many fixtures). Two options: inline submodules stay (a module is one file or one inline block), or every module is its own file and the inline form is refused. Recommendation: every module its own file, as OD9's answer reads; the specification's examples and the fixtures move their inline modules into project examples when exit 2q migrates the corpus. Austral has one module per file pair and no inline modules Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): no inline `mod m { }`: one file per module | the current compiler accepts inline submodules; MOD-7 (pending, R-0556) records OD9 without settling this; OPEN-33 | R-0556; the specification's examples and the fixture corpus | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): No inline `mod`; one file per module |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:528:| OD34 | <a id="od34"></a>A struct literal inside a call's argument list, an index or an array literal within an `if`, `while` or `match` head (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD16): whether it counts as parenthesized under SYN-4. Two readings: only a grouped expression `( expr )` parenthesizes, so `if same(p, P { v: 1 }) { ... }` is refused; or any enclosing bracket does, so it is accepted (Rust's reading). Recommendation: any enclosing bracket, since inside a call's parentheses the head's `{` cannot be meant, and refusing it would require parentheses inside parentheses Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): only an enclosing `(` or `[` parenthesizes, stated as one invariant: in a condition head, a `{` at bracket depth zero opens the block | not in the record; the current compiler accepts it (the uppercase heuristic reads a struct literal anywhere in a head) | bug 318's repair (SYN-4); V1c | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Only enclosing `(`/`[` parenthesize; `{` at depth 0 opens the block |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:529:| OD35 | <a id="od35"></a>How an array satisfies a `Destroy` bound (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD28, which allows either form): one impl written in std for `[T; N]` with `T: Destroy`, or one language impl that is its own specification entry. Recommendation: the language impl, since an array's destruction is its elements' in order and std cannot write an impl generic over the length without const generics Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): a language impl `[T; N]: Destroy` when `T: Destroy`, elements destroyed in index order, with its own identity in coherence | the current compiler counts an array of destroyable elements as `Destroy` with no impl (bug 324) | bug 324's repair (TRAIT-10) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language impl `[T; N]: Destroy` when `T: Destroy`, index order |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:530:| OD36 | <a id="od36"></a>The spelling of the explicit non-consuming place form that opens a `Heap` or `HeapArray` (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD30, which requires one and keeps `*` as the consuming move-out). Options: a borrow through the cell (`(&h).f`, `(&mut h).f`), a named projection (`h.open().f`), or a dedicated operator. Recommendation: the borrow form, which needs no new syntax and matches OD32's explicit receiver borrow Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): only through a borrow block (`borrow h as r { r.f }`), no `(&h).f`; specify the result type for `Heap<T>`, `&Heap<T>` and `&mut Heap<T>`, one heap layer per block (MEM-4), and `HeapArray` elements | the current compiler opens one heap layer implicitly (`h.f` on `Heap<S>`, bug 326) | bug 326's repair (MEM-4) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Only through a borrow block; no `(&h).f` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:531:| OD37 | <a id="od37"></a>The names of the pointer difference and ordering functions (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD26). Options: `ptr::diff(p, q) -> Int` and `ptr::cmp(p, q) -> Ordering` beside `ptr::offset`; or no ordering at all, only `ptr::addr(p)` (via `ptr::expose`) compared as integers. Recommendation: `ptr::diff` and `ptr::cmp`, both trusted, each with its provenance premise (same allocation) stated as an obligation; Austral has no named pointer-difference or ordering functions, and its `Pointer` and `Address` types are compared with the ordering operators Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): one trusted `ptr::diff` (same-allocation premise), ordering read from its sign, no `ptr::cmp`; specify the representability of its result, one-past pointers included (Q8 is marked agreed in the picks; ROADMAP records no owner answer for it) | trusted code compares raw pointers with `==` and `<` today (bug 322) | bug 322's repair (TYPE-6, TRUST-1) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One trusted `ptr::diff`, ordering from its sign, no `ptr::cmp` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:532:| OD38 | <a id="od38"></a>The root resource API (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD8). Options: `fn main(root: Root) -> u8` with trusted splitting functions (`root.split_alloc()`, `root.split_console()`, ...) returning linear tokens; or a fixed set of token parameters `main` may declare (`fn main(alloc: Alloc, io: Console)`). Recommendation: the single `Root` value, as Austral's `RootCapability`, split by trusted functions the wrapper gate lists Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): `fn main(root: Root) -> u8` (or Unit), `Root` split into linear tokens by trusted functions; specify the split functions and how leftover authority is consumed | `main` takes no parameters today (STMT-7) | R-0557 (TRUST-9) and STMT-7 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Superseded by the authority decision of 2026-11-01: there is no `Root` type; `main`'s parameters are the target's singletons, hosted `fn main() with(...) -> u8` and freestanding `fn main(uart: Uart, arena: Arena) with(Device, Alloc) -> u8`; revised 2026-11-02: `main` receives the target's grants and returns `u8` only ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:533:| OD39 | <a id="od39"></a>The interface artifact format (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD9). Options: a generated `.coni` text file per module beside the build output; a JSON interface in the build directory; or the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source. Recommendation: rendered Concrete source (declarations without bodies), deterministic and reviewable as a diff Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source without bodies, generated by the compiler, with an API-diff gate; it also carries private layout, impl methods, attributes, constants, capabilities and generic bodies, and is not read as FFI-6 bodyless foreign bindings | the compiler has the declaration-level `FileSummary` and `--report interface`, but no persisted per-module interface artifact and no API-diff gate of the proposed form | R-0556 (MOD-7) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `pub` decls rendered as Concrete source, compiler-generated, API-diff gate |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:534:| OD40 | <a id="od40"></a>PAT-9 and the fallback arm of a desugared `if let`, `let ... else` or `while let` (added 2026-10-29, while fixing bug 317). Options: the fallback arm is not a written arm and is not judged; or it is judged, so `if let` over a one-variant enum is refused as unreachable. Recommendation: not judged, since the source has no arm to delete Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): moot if OD52 removes the desugared forms | `if let` over a one-variant enum is accepted | bug 317's repair (PAT-9) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Moot: OD52 removed `if let`/`while let`/`let...else` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:535:| OD41 | <a id="od41"></a>PAT-9 and integer ranges that span their type (added 2026-10-29, while fixing bug 317). Options: a catch-all after ranges covering the whole integer type is not refused (PAT-2 still requires it); or it is refused and PAT-2 is relaxed to accept ranges that cover the type. Recommendation: not refused, keeping PAT-2 as decided Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): close as range-specific under OD56, recording in PAT-2 and PAT-9 that an integer match still needs a catch-all and that listing every literal is not coverage | such a match is accepted | bug 317's repair (PAT-9, PAT-2) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Close as range-specific; integer matches keep a required catch-all, listing every literal is not coverage |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:537:| OD43 | <a id="od43"></a>How a type position resolves a name (added 2026-10-29, from the spec gap bug 229's ruling found). Options: (a) the current compiler's rules: type positions search the type namespace only, a user `enum Option` is always the builtin, and a parent sees its child's newtypes; (b) a user declaration shadows a builtin of the same name, with a warning; (c) a user type may not take a builtin type's name (refused). Recommendation: (c), which removes the shadowing question entirely; Austral reserves its builtin type names the same way Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (c) refused, for builtin-name collisions only, with the complete reserved set published (also for aliases and import renames); this answer would not retire OD43, whose type-versus-value lookup and child-module questions stay open in it. A user `struct Copy HeapArray` is treated as linear today | the current compiler follows (a) | MOD-1; bug 229's record | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse builtin-name collisions, publish reserved set. Completed 2026-11-02 by the owner: one namespace per module, and a child module's declarations only through `child::T` or an import ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-namespace)) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:538:| OD44 | <a id="od44"></a>Two packages of one program that define the same top-level module name (added 2026-10-29, from bug 074's ruling). Options: (a) refused when the project loads, naming both packages (the current compiler); (b) module paths are qualified by their package, so both coexist. Recommendation: (a) until packages depend on packages (R-0440), then revisit with (b) Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) refused at load, naming both packages, until packages depend on packages (R-0440) | the current compiler refuses (bug 074) | MOD-1, MOD-4; R-0440 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse at load, naming both, until R-0440 |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:540:| OD46 | <a id="od46"></a>Generic type-argument inference (added 2026-10-29, from bug 308's ruling). Main today binds each type parameter from the types its peek (`PeekType`) computes for the arguments given for it, a value `if` or `match` included since bug 308, but answers `.placeholder` for a generic call's result and some arms with binders, and refuses a parameter no argument determines; it never infers from the expected result type. Options: (a) specify the current compiler's peek-based rule, listing the forms it covers; (b) bind from the checked type of every argument, whatever its form; (c) also infer from the expected type at the call site. Recommendation: (b), local and predictable, with explicit type arguments for the rest; Austral infers type parameters from the arguments, and a `let` declaration's type can disambiguate a return-type-polymorphic call Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (b) from checked argument types only, never from the expected result; arguments that need an expected type are refused, not defaulted | the current compiler is (a) without the list | TYPE-13, TYPE-15; bug 308's record | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): From checked argument types only |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:541:| OD47 | <a id="od47"></a>Loops: `while` and a counted range loop (added 2026-10-29) | the current compiler has `while` and the C-style `for (init; cond; step)`, `for (cond)` and `for { }`; bug 331 | exit 2q; R-0499; V1a's loops and exits | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: (1) the C-style `for` is removed in every form; (2) loops are `while cond { ... }` and `for i from a until b { ... }`: half-open (`a`, ..., `b - 1`, empty when `a >= b`), the index immutable in the body, `a` and `b` evaluated once before the first iteration, the index of the bounds' integer type (both bounds of one type), `break` and `continue` as usual (`continue` advances to the next index), `from` and `until` reserved (whether reserved or contextual was reopened by the reviews of that day: OD50), LL(1) as `for` IDENT `from` expr `until` expr block with the restricted head expression for `b` (OD16); (3) in Core the counted loop desugars to the one `while` form, carrying its termination measure `b - i`. Now STMT-6@3, STMT-11@2 and STMT-12 (pending, bug 331) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:542:| OD48 | <a id="od48"></a>The index type of a counted loop over two literals (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD47). Options: (a) refused, as OD31 refuses any literal with no expected type (write `for i from 0 until n` with a typed bound, or annotate); (b) the index is `Int` there. Recommendation: (a), consistent with OD31 Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) refused, as OD31 refuses any literal with no expected type; a typed bound supplies the other bound's type, in either order | the counted loop is not in the current compiler | bug 331's repair (STMT-6) | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refused, as OD31 |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:544:| OD50 | <a id="od50"></a>Reserved or contextual `from` and `until` (added 2026-10-29, from the reviews of OD47). Options: (a) reserved words everywhere, as OD47 first said: `let from: Int` is refused and the lexer has two more keywords; (b) contextual keywords, meaningful only between `for IDENT` and the block, so existing names stay legal. Recommendation: (a), one lexical rule and no context-dependent tokens, at the cost of two names; Austral reserves its loop words Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) reserved everywhere | the current compiler has neither word (bug 331) | bug 331's repair; SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Reserved everywhere |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:545:| OD51 | <a id="od51"></a>Reverse, stepped and collection iteration (added 2026-10-29, from the reviews of OD47). Options: (a) none: the counted loop is ascending by one, everything else is a `while`; (b) add a descending form (`for i from a down until b`) and a step (`by k`) later, each its own decision; (c) add iteration over a collection. Recommendation: (a) now, revisiting (b) when a workload needs it; a `while` keeps the proof story at one loop construct Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) none now; each a later decision when a workload needs it | the current compiler has no counted loop (bug 331) | STMT-6 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): None now |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:546:| OD52 | <a id="od52"></a>`if let`, `while let` and `let ... else` (added 2026-10-30, item S1 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `if let`, `while let` and `let ... else` stay in the language or are removed in favour of `match`. Options: (a) keep the three forms (PAT-6, their desugaring to `match`); (b) remove them, `match` being the only refutable pattern form. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) remove. Austral: none of the three; `case` is the only refutable form | all three are accepted and desugar to a `match` with a catch-all arm (PAT-6) | PAT-6, PAT-8, PAT-9, OPEN-40, SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:547:| OD53 | <a id="od53"></a>Struct update `..base` (added 2026-10-30, item S2 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the struct update form `S { a: 1, ..base }` stays in the language. Options: (a) keep it; (b) remove it, every field written. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) remove. Austral: no struct update; every field is written | accepted (TYPE-7, OWN-15's value flow for `..base`) | TYPE-7, OWN-15 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:548:| OD54 | <a id="od54"></a>`newtype` and `.0` (added 2026-10-30, item S3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `newtype` declarations and the `.0` projection stay, or a one-field struct with a private field replaces them. Options: (a) keep `newtype` and `.0`; (b) remove both, using a one-field struct whose field is private to its module. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) remove. Austral: no newtype; a type is made opaque by the module interface hiding its definition (a record being one possible definition) | `newtype` declarations and `.0` are accepted; `unwrap(id)` is an intrinsic | TYPE-7, TYPE-11, ARITH-5 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:549:| OD55 | <a id="od55"></a>Match guards (added 2026-10-30, item S10 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether match guards (`pattern if cond =>`) stay in the language. Options: (a) keep them (PAT-4); (b) remove them, the condition written inside the arm. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) remove. Austral: no guards | guards are accepted and read-only (PAT-4) | PAT-4, PAT-2, PAT-9 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:550:| OD56 | <a id="od56"></a>Range patterns (added 2026-10-30, item S11 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether integer range patterns (`1..=9 =>`) stay in the language (this amends OD19). Options: (a) keep them (PAT-7); (b) remove them, the comparison written in the arm or an `if` chain. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) remove; then close OPEN-41 as range-specific, recording in PAT-2 and PAT-9 that an integer match still needs a catch-all and that listing every literal is not coverage, so that catch-all is never unreachable. Austral: no range patterns | range patterns are accepted (PAT-7) | PAT-7, PAT-2, PAT-9, OPEN-41, OD19 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove; close OD41 as range-specific |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:551:| OD57 | <a id="od57"></a>The region name of a borrow block (added 2026-10-30, item S12b of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a borrow block keeps its region name (`borrow x as r in R { }`) or drops `in R`. Options: (a) keep `in R`; (b) drop it, the block itself being the region. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) drop `in R`. Austral: the borrow statement names its region (`borrow x as r in R do ... end`); anonymous borrows `&x` in call arguments name none | the region name is written and has no other use in the language | REF-8, REF-11, SCOPE-D1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Drop `in R` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:552:| OD58 | <a id="od58"></a>Borrow blocks stay (added 2026-10-30, item S12 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether borrow blocks (`borrow x as r in R { }`, as written today) stay the way to hold a reference across statements. Options: (a) keep them; (b) remove them, references living only within one call's arguments. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) keep (recorded as already agreed in the picks; no owner record yet). Austral: the borrow statement is the way to hold a reference across statements | borrow blocks are accepted (REF-8, REF-11) | REF-6, REF-8, REF-11, OPEN-36 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Keep |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:553:| OD59 | <a id="od59"></a>One way to consume a `Heap` (added 2026-10-30, item S7 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Which operations consume a `Heap<T>`: the move-out `*h` (OD30), `destroy(h)`, `free(h)` or a new `take(h)`. Options: (a) keep `*h` and `free(h)` as today; (b) `*h` as move-out-and-free and `destroy(h)` extended to a Heap, `free` deleted, no `take`; (c) `take(h)` replaces `*h` as the move-out, `destroy(h)` extended to a Heap, `free` deleted. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b): `*h` and a `destroy(h)` extended to a Heap, no `free`, no `take` (a `take` would be another spelling of `*h`). Austral: not checked | `*h` moves out and frees and `free(h)` frees the cell and returns its value (MEM-5); `destroy(h)` on a Heap is refused (E0272: destroy() requires a named type) | MEM-5, OWN-13, OPEN-36 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `*h` + `destroy(h)`, no `free`, no `take` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:555:| OD61 | <a id="od61"></a>One coordinated removal list (added 2026-10-30, item review new 7 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the decided and proposed removals (inline `mod`, the C-style `for`, `if let` / `while let` / `let ... else`, `newtype`, `..base`, range patterns, guards, `in R`, `break <expr>` and the `trusted mod` marker) are migrated as one coordinated list. Options: (a) one list, migrated in exit 2q's single transition; (b) each removal on its own schedule. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) one list, including dropping TRUST-6's `trusted mod` marker, which S14 removes (S14 is marked agreed in the picks; ROADMAP records no owner answer for it). Austral: not applicable | TRUST-6 (pending) still requires the `trusted mod` marker, which S14's pick would drop (no owner answer is recorded for S14) | TRUST-6, STMT-6, PAT-6, PAT-7, PAT-4, TYPE-7, MOD-4, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One list (incl. dropping `trusted mod`) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:556:| OD62 | <a id="od62"></a>Unused values and variables (added 2026-10-30, item Q3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether an unused local variable or parameter is refused (Go's rule) and whether an unused `Result` or `Option` may be dropped by `discard`. Options: (a) refuse only an unused `Result` or `Option` (OWN-6); (b) Go's rule: any unused local or parameter is refused (an intentionally unused parameter is named `_`), and `discard` refuses `Result` and `Option`. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b), stated as one invariant: every name a body binds is used or is `_` (a bodyless declaration has no body, so needs no exception); `discard` and wildcard drops refuse `Result` and `Option`, Copy or not; to clarify: `_` as a loop index, and an unused `&mut` binding under REF-10. Austral: not checked | unused Copy locals and parameters are accepted; OWN-6 refuses an unused fallible result | OWN-5, OWN-6, OWN-7, REF-10 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Every bound name used or `_`; `discard` refuses Result/Option |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:557:| OD63 | <a id="od63"></a>Float literals take their expected type (added 2026-10-30, item Q4 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a float literal takes the float type its position expects and is refused where nothing is expected, as ARITH-2@2 states under OD31's note that float literals get an explicit rule under the same principle. Options: (a) yes, as ARITH-2@2 states for integers and floats alike; (b) a float literal defaults to `f64`. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a). Austral: not checked | `let x = 1.5;` is accepted as `Float64` (ARITH-2@2 is partial, bug 327) | ARITH-2, ARITH-7 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Expected type, refused where none |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:558:| OD64 | <a id="od64"></a>Raw pointers derived from a borrow (added 2026-10-30, item G19 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What a raw pointer derived from a borrow may do: whether it is valid only while the borrow lives, may escape the borrow block, may be passed to C, and how extern parameters say so. Options: (a) valid only while the borrow lives and never escaping it; (b) the operational loan rules of REF-14 only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): no returning, no escaping storage (a local `let` inside the block stays allowed), no converting to an integer and no capturing in an outer `defer`; borrow-derived pointers go only to call-only `extern` parameters; C uses it only during a call (memory C keeps must come from an allocation with its own owner); `extern` parameters say call-only (the default) or kept; field pointers come from the same borrow of the whole owner; deferred calls in the block run before the borrow ends; provenance survives copies, offsets, field pointers, helpers and indirect calls; a shared borrow never yields write access; `ptr::expose` is refused on such a pointer; the annotation's syntax gets an LL(1) check. Austral: not checked | no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity (OD15); REF-14 bounds aliases by their loan | REF-14, TRUST-1, TRUST-3, FFI-3, OWN-12 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Valid only inside the borrow; call-only extern params |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:559:| OD65 | <a id="od65"></a>Empty structs (added 2026-10-30, item review new 1 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a struct with no fields (`struct S {}`) is allowed, as a unit-like type for capability and root tokens, or refused. Options: (a) allowed; (b) refused. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: not checked | the parser accepts `struct S {}` and `S {}`; the grammar requires at least one field | TYPE-7, TYPE-16, TRUST-9, OPEN-38 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Allow |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:560:| OD66 | <a id="od66"></a>The unit literal `()` (added 2026-10-30, item review new 2 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `()` is the unit value's literal, given that the grammar has no production for it. Options: (a) add `()` to the grammar; (b) no unit literal, Unit produced only by a statement or a Unit call. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: the unit value is written `nil` | the grammar has no production for `()` | SYN-1, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Add `()` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:561:| OD67 | <a id="od67"></a>Locals that reuse a top-level name (added 2026-10-30, item review new 4 (SCOPE-4) of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a local binding may reuse the name of a top-level function, an import alias or an intrinsic (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.4 also lists constants). Options: (a) allowed, as today; (b) refused, as SCOPE-1 refuses shadowing a local. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: not checked | allowed | SCOPE-1, SCOPE-2, MOD-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:562:| OD68 | <a id="od68"></a>Failure inside a deferred call (added 2026-10-30, item review new 5 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What OWN-12's text says about a failure inside a deferred call, given that an abort runs no deferred call (STMT-1, STMT-2). Options: (a) OWN-12 states that a trap inside a deferred call ends the program and runs no further deferred call, as STMT-2 and OD10 say for every abort; (b) the remaining deferred calls still run after a trapping one, which would amend STMT-2 and OD10. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: not checked | failure is abort only (STMT-2); OWN-12's rationale leaves the case open | OWN-12, STMT-1, STMT-2, STMT-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Trap ends the program, no further deferred call |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:563:| OD69 | <a id="od69"></a>What "the remaining locals are destroyed" means (added 2026-10-30, item review new 6 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether STMT-11's last exit step ("the remaining locals are destroyed") means storage teardown only, with no synthesized `Destroy` call, as OWN-13 requires. Options: (a) storage teardown only, no call, as OWN-13 requires; (b) a remaining local whose type implements `Destroy` is destroyed by a synthesized call, which would amend OWN-13. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: no implicit destruction | nothing is destroyed implicitly (OWN-13) | STMT-11, OWN-13 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Storage teardown only |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:564:| OD70 | <a id="od70"></a>Struct fields with no comma (added 2026-10-30, item P2 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether struct fields written without a separating comma (`struct S { a: i32 b: i32 }`) are refused. Options: (a) refused, the grammar wins; (b) the grammar adds the form. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) refuse. Austral: separators are required | the parser accepts it | SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:565:| OD71 | <a id="od71"></a>Enum variants with no comma (added 2026-10-30, item P3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether enum variants written without a separating comma are refused. Options: (a) refused; (b) the grammar adds the form. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) refuse. Austral: separators are required | the parser accepts it | SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:566:| OD72 | <a id="od72"></a>An enum with no variants (added 2026-10-30, item P4 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether an empty enum `enum E { }` is refused or is the uninhabited type. Options: (a) refused; (b) the grammar adds it as the uninhabited type. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) refuse until a workload needs an uninhabited type. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts it | SYN-1, PAT-2 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse until a workload needs it |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:567:| OD73 | <a id="od73"></a>Reserved words used as identifiers (added 2026-10-30, item P5 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `cap` and `type`, which the lexer reserves, are refused as identifiers where the parser lets them through. Options: (a) refused, reserved words stay reserved; (b) the grammar lets them through there. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) refuse. Austral: keywords are reserved | `expectIdent` accepts `cap` (and `type`) as a variant name | SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:568:| OD74 | <a id="od74"></a>A raw pointer type with no qualifier (added 2026-10-30, item P6 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a bare `*T` is refused or read as `*mut T`. Options: (a) refused, `*const` or `*mut` required; (b) the grammar adds `*T` as `*mut T`. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) refuse; the qualifier is the safety-relevant part. Austral: not applicable | the parser reads `*T` as `*mut T` | SYN-1, TRUST-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse; qualifier required |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:569:| OD75 | <a id="od75"></a>An empty type parameter list (added 2026-10-30, item P7 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `impl<> S` (an empty type parameter list) is refused. Options: (a) refused; (b) the grammar adds it. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) refuse. Austral: not applicable | the parser accepts it | SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:571:| OD77 | <a id="od77"></a>Resync the grammar to the decided language (added 2026-10-30, item B of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `grammar/concrete.ebnf` is resynchronized for the items the language already decided: `E#V` becomes `E::V`, binding renaming, a function body with no trailing value and `;` for bodyless functions, no generic trait methods, assignment targets are places, unknown attribute names refused, and no `break <expr>`. Options: (a) approve the resync, as actual productions with a factored `place` prefix for assignment; (b) defer it, the divergences staying recorded in grammar/conformance_known.tsv (the decided language does not change either way). Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) approve, with actual productions, a `place` prefix and `break <expr>` removed. Austral: not applicable | the grammar is stale on these items (grammar/conformance_known.tsv) | SYN-1, PAT-1, STMT-6, STMT-8, OWN-8, FFI-6 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Approve resync with actual productions |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:578:| OD84 | <a id="od84"></a>Struct destructuring `let` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C7 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains struct destructuring `let S { a } = e;`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: destructuring `let` of records exists (not checked) | the parser accepts it (PAT-5) | PAT-5, SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Add struct-destructuring `let` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:579:| OD85 | <a id="od85"></a>Variant destructuring with `else` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C8 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains `let E::V { a } = e else { ... };`, which OD52's pick would remove. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): follows OD52 (removed under its pick). Austral: no `let ... else` | the parser accepts it (PAT-8) | PAT-8, OPEN-52, SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove (follows OD52) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:580:| OD86 | <a id="od86"></a>The body of an unbraced match arm (added 2026-10-30, item R1 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar narrows an unbraced match-arm body to an expression, a block or `return`, which is what the parser accepts (`continue;` as an unbraced body is refused). Options: (a) narrow the grammar; (b) widen the parser. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) narrow. Austral: not applicable | the parser accepts an expression, a block or `return` as an unbraced arm body and refuses other statements | SYN-1, PAT-3 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Narrow |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:584:| OD90 | <a id="od90"></a>`struct Copy <T>` and contextual keywords (added 2026-10-30, item Copy of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `Copy` stays a contextual keyword, with the LL(1) checkers taught to model it, or becomes a reserved word (the other unreserved words, `self`, `repr`, `packed` and `align`, stay as they are unless decided separately). Options: (a) reserve `Copy` as a keyword, refusing it as an identifier; (b) keep it contextual, the LL(1) checkers taught to model a quoted unreserved terminal as also IDENT, each conflict found listed for the owner (letters as in the divergence report). Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) reserve `Copy`: one lexical rule, consistent with OD50 (reserved `from` and `until`) and OD43 (builtin names refused as user names). Austral: its keywords are reserved | contextual; the `struct Copy <T>` conflict is invisible to the checkers' token model | SYN-1, OWN-D1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Reserve `Copy` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:585:| OD91 | <a id="od91"></a>A cast followed by `<` (added 2026-10-30, item LL(1) type\|5.2 of grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv). Question: How `x as T < y` parses, given that `<` after a cast's type name may start type arguments or be the less-than operator. Options: (a) a cast's type takes no type arguments unless parenthesized (`x as (T<U>)`), so `<` is less-than; (b) the parser's reading: `<` always starts type arguments, so the comparison needs parentheses (`(x as T) < y`); (c) refuse `<` directly after a cast's type and require parentheses either way. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (in the LL(1) ledger, not the picks). Austral: a conversion is the cast expression `e : T`, which takes no type arguments after it, so the conflict does not arise | the parser always reads type arguments, so `x as T < y` is refused (grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv, row type\|5.2) | SYN-1, SYN-2, ARITH-5 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `x as T < y` is less-than; a generic cast type needs parentheses `x as (Vec<T>)` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:586:| OD92 | <a id="od92"></a>A capability set inferred from callbacks (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a combinator may write its capability set as that of its callbacks (`with(f)`), the checker computing the union of the sets of the callbacks its body calls. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: no such form; `with(f)` reads `f` as a capability name (an unresolved one, which MOD-1 refuses and the current compiler accepts: bug 337), so a combinator names a capability parameter (`fn map<T, U, cap C>(.., f: fn(&T) with(C) -> U) with(C)`). The callable-values design (2026-06-12, [CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md) §6) recorded the shortcut as specified and not built, after [research/language/capability-polymorphism.md](../../research/language/capability-polymorphism.md); no roadmap item tracks it. Options: (a) no shortcut, combinators name capability parameters; (b) add it, as set propagation and not row unification. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-92 | see OPEN-92 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No shortcut; name a capability parameter |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:587:| OD93 | <a id="od93"></a>Widening a function value's capability set (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a function value converts to a function type whose capability set contains its own, implicitly by subset or by an explicit conversion. Bears on TYPE-4, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: refused (E0220): `fn(Int) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int`, nor `with(File)` a `with(Std)`, and there is no explicit conversion; a capability-polymorphic parameter accepts either by instantiating its variable. The callable-values design (§10) says there is no implicit widening, and that "widening is explicit or via lattice subsumption only", with a lattice example (`Concurrent ⊇ Async`) whose names are not capabilities; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES R7 keeps handle types invariant, as TYPE-4 does. Options: (a) exact types, as in the current compiler; (b) subsumption into a larger set; (c) an explicit conversion. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-93 | see OPEN-93 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Exact types **Canonical current capability-set rule: authority rule 4; this earlier exact-types answer refers to it.** |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:588:| OD94 | <a id="od94"></a>Names in `with(...)` that are not capabilities (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a name in `with(...)` that is not a capability, a capability alias or a capability variable in scope is refused. Bears on TRUST-D1, TYPE-D1, TRUST-4. The current compiler: accepted as a capability of its own: `fn main() with(Foo) -> Int` compiles and runs, and a call to a function that requires `Foo` is refused only in a caller that does not list it (E0520). The compiler's `validCaps`, TRUST-4's rationale and [SAFETY.md](../verification/SAFETY.md) name nine capabilities. Options: (a) refuse an unknown name; (b) an open vocabulary. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | [MOD-1](modules.md#MOD-1) refuses the name; the current compiler accepts it (bug 337) | MOD-1@1 | withdrawn 2026-10-30: not an owner question; MOD-1@1 refuses the name (bug 337) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:589:| OD95 | <a id="od95"></a>What `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` mean (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: What authority each of `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` names. Bears on TRUST-D1, TRUST-4. The current compiler: no document states them; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES §2 defined `Console`, `File` and `Network`, and SAFETY.md `Random` (TRUST-D1). std requires `Time` for its clock and sleep functions, `Env` for environment variables and for `std.net`'s `bind` and `connect`, `Process` for exit, spawn, wait and kill, and `Alloc` for heap allocation. Options: state each as std uses it, or narrow or split it (std's `Env` on socket functions is the case to settle). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-95 | see OPEN-95 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): State each; move socket `bind`/`connect` from `Env` to `Network` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:591:| OD97 | <a id="od97"></a>A build profile that omits or demands an overflow check (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a build profile may omit the run-time check of an integer operation that no proof discharges, or refuse such an operation at compile time. Bears on ARITH-3, ARITH-9. The current compiler: every profile emits every check, and no option turns one off; ARITH-3's rationale says a profile changes reports, never code generation, and OD10 decided that arithmetic is checked "by default". The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §3.1 instead let a release profile omit a check that is "explicitly configured off and reported", and a provable or high-integrity profile refuse an unproved overflow site. Options: (a) every profile keeps every check that no discharged proof removes; (b) a release profile may turn checks off when configured and reported, never wrapping; (c) as (a), and a profile may also refuse an operation whose no-overflow obligation is not discharged. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-97 | see OPEN-97 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep every check; a profile may refuse undischarged ops |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:592:| OD98 | <a id="od98"></a>Pointer and reference conversions written with `as` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Which conversions between raw pointers and references `as` writes (`&x as *const T`, `p as &T`, `p as &mut T`, `p as *mut U`). Bears on TRUST-1, TRUST-3, REF-5, ARITH-5. The current compiler: `&x as *const T` is accepted in safe code, `p as &T` from a raw pointer is accepted in trusted or `Unsafe` code (the binary and `--interp` agree), and a reference cast between different pointee types (`a as &Int` with `a: &A`) is refused (E0553). No entry states these: TRUST-3 forms a reference from a raw pointer with `&*p`, and REF-5 lists borrowing and projection as the ways a reference is formed. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8 kept `as` "the spelling of pointer and reference casts (`as *const T`, `as *mut T`, `as &K`, `as &mut V`)", 281 lines of std. Options: (a) `as` keeps every one of them; (b) `as` converts only a reference to a raw pointer, and a reference is formed from a raw pointer only by `&*p` (TRUST-3); (c) pointer conversions become named functions. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-98 | see OPEN-98 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Reference to raw pointer conversion only in trusted/Unsafe code (equal to Austral) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:593:| OD99 | <a id="od99"></a>The names of the named conversions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: The spellings of the named conversions of ARITH-5, ARITH-10 and ARITH-11 (`try_convert`, `truncate`, `reinterpret`, the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions, and the float roundings `trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`). Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10, ARITH-11. The current compiler: none of them exists (E0101). ARITH-5's sentence names `truncate` and `reinterpret`, while the archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 calls its spellings indicative, to be fixed by the API's owner; it names the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions by their rounding only. Options: (a) the spellings above, as builtins; (b) other names, or std functions rather than builtins. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-99 | see OPEN-99 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): These spellings, as builtins |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:594:| OD100 | <a id="od100"></a>Comparisons on a type parameter (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a comparison on a value of a `Copy`-bounded type parameter is accepted and checked again at each instantiation, or refused in the generic body. Bears on TYPE-6, TYPE-9, PASS-1, OPEN-42. The current compiler: `a < b` with `a, b: T` and `T: Copy` is accepted, and an instantiation at a type with no comparison is refused after monomorphization (E0229, `post-mono`); without the `Copy` bound it is refused in the body (E0229, `check`). TYPE-9's rationale says a generic body is checked once, from its bounds, which a per-instantiation check departs from. Options: (a) keep the current compiler's per-instantiation check; (b) refuse a comparison on a type parameter until a bound states it (a comparison trait); (c) refuse it. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md §8a (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-100 | see OPEN-100 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse until a bound states it |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:595:| OD101 | <a id="od101"></a>What a trap writes (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a run-time trap writes a message naming the operation, its operands and its source location before the process ends, and in what form. Bears on STMT-2, STMT-7, ARITH-3. The current compiler: the binary writes nothing and ends by `SIGABRT`, and `--interp` reports a run-time error naming the trap (`arithmetic overflow (checked +)`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §9.2 described a message `concrete: arithmetic overflow in function 'f' at file:line` with the operation, the operands and the policy. Options: (a) the binary writes nothing; (b) it writes one line naming the trap and its location; (c) the §9.2 form. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-101 | see OPEN-101 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): One line naming the trap and location |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:596:| OD102 | <a id="od102"></a>`Float32` and `Char` as type spellings (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether `Float32` and `Char` are other spellings of `f32` and `char`, as `Float64` and `Bool` are of `f64` and `bool`. Bears on TYPE-19, SYN-3. The current compiler: the parser maps `Float64` and `Bool` to `f64` and `bool` but not `Float32` or `Char`, so `fn g(x: f32) -> Float32 { return x; }` is refused (E0220) while the resolver's `tyFromName` maps all four. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §1 names `Float32`/`f32` as two spellings. Options: (a) `Float32` and `Char` become spellings of `f32` and `char`; (b) neither, keeping the current compiler's `Float64` and `Bool`; (c) `Float32` and `Char` decided separately. Whether every capitalized spelling (`Int`, `Uint`, `Float64`, `Bool`) is removed is a separate removal question, not asked here. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-102 | see OPEN-102 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No `Float32` or `Char` aliases. Completed 2026-11-02 by the owner: the aliases `Int`, `Uint`, `Float64` and `Bool` are removed too ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-aliases)) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:597:| OD103 | <a id="od103"></a>`as` between reference types (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing MEMORY_SEMANTICS). Question: Whether `as` may convert a reference type to a reference type, given that REF-5 lists borrowing and projecting as the only operations that form a reference. Bears on REF-5, REF-6, ARITH-5, TRUST-4. The current compiler: an identity cast between equal reference types is accepted and denotes the operand's place (`read(r as &Int)` with `r: &Int`, and `read(&v as &Int)`, exit 42 on both consumers); every other cast between reference types, `&mut Int as &Int` included, is E0553 at core-check under ARITH-5's tag, whose sentence covers only numeric, `bool` and `char` conversions; a cast from a reference to a raw pointer is allowed (TRUST-4). The design record (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.3) listed a cast of a traced reference binding (`r as &U`) among the reference-forming operations. Options: (a) refuse `as` between reference types; (b) allow the identity cast as a no-op on the operand's place. Conversions between a reference and a raw pointer are OPEN-98's, not this question's. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the MEMORY_SEMANTICS absorption, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-103 | see OPEN-103 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:598:| OD104 | <a id="od104"></a>A `#[variant]` written on a counted loop (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the OD47 loop rules). Question: whether a `#[variant]` attribute written on a counted loop is refused, checked like any loop variant, or ignored. Options: (a) refused, the loop's own measure being its only variant; (b) allowed and checked as a loop variant (CON-2); (c) allowed and ignored. No pick given | the counted loop is not in the current compiler (bug 331) | STMT-12, CON-2 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:599:| OD105 | <a id="od105"></a>Where a counted loop's `#[invariant]` is checked (added 2026-10-30, from the same review). Question: where an `#[invariant]` on a counted loop is checked, given that CON-2 checks a loop invariant before the loop and after every iteration. Options: (a) as CON-2 says for every loop; (b) at each entry to the body, so nothing is checked when the loop runs no iteration. No pick given; (b) needs proof requirement 2 | the counted loop is not in the current compiler (bug 331) | STMT-12, CON-2 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): As CON-2: before the loop and after every iteration |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:600:| OD106 | <a id="od106"></a>The result type of a checked conversion (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a checked conversion (`try_convert`) reports a value it cannot preserve as `Option::None` or as a `Result` error carrying the reason. Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10. The current compiler: no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 wrote `try_convert::<T>(x) -> Option<T>` "(or a `Result` with a reason)" and left the choice to the API's owner. Options: (a) `Option<T>`; (b) a `Result` whose error names the reason (out of range, not integral, NaN, infinite, not representable). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500) | ARITH-5, ARITH-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `Result<T, OutOfRange>` (failure, not absence) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:601:| OD107 | <a id="od107"></a>What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether each float-to-integer rounding operation (`trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`) gives a value or an error for NaN, an infinity or a result outside the target type's range, and which value. Bears on ARITH-1, ARITH-11. The current compiler: the rounding operations do not exist (R-0500); a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range, natively and in `--interp`. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 required a stated result for every input, "either as a value or as an error". Options: (a) an error, as `try_convert` reports; (b) a value: the nearest value of the type for an out-of-range input or an infinity, and 0 for NaN; (c) a trap, as `as` does today. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the rounding operations do not exist; a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range | ARITH-1, ARITH-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): An error, as `try_convert` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:602:| OD108 | <a id="od108"></a>A capability listed twice in a set (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a capability set that lists one capability twice (`with(Console, Console)`) is the same set as the one that lists it once, is refused, or is a different set. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4. The current compiler: accepted where it is written, and a different type: `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console, Console) -> Int`, in either direction (E0220), because `CapSet.normalize` sorts a set and does not remove a repeated name. The callable-values design ([CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md)) calls the capabilities of a function type a set. Options: (a) the same set; (b) refuse a repeated capability where it is written; (c) a different set, as in the current compiler. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | accepted, and a different set: `with(Console, Console)` is not `with(Console)` (E0220) | TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse a repeated capability |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:603:| OD109 | <a id="od109"></a>Specializations that differ only in a function type's capabilities (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether the symbol of a generic function's instantiation includes the capability set of a function-type argument, so that two instantiations that differ only in that set have distinct symbols. Bears on TYPE-17, TYPE-18. The current compiler: one: `pick<T: Copy>` instantiated at `fn(Int) -> Int` and at `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` emits the single function `pick_for_Fn_T_Int_to_Int_E`, because Mono's `tyToSuffix` drops a function type's capability set while it keeps a struct's capability arguments (E0809's history), and the program runs correctly since capabilities have no run-time representation (TYPE-18). The archived [HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md](../archive/design-records/HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md) R8 required collision-free symbol identity across instantiations and left code sharing to its own argument. Options: (a) the set is part of the symbol, as for struct capability arguments; (b) it is not, one symbol serving both, stated as erasure with its correctness argument. Whether two symbols share one body stays an implementation choice (TYPE-18). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | one specialization: Mono's symbol drops a function type's capability set | TYPE-17, TYPE-18 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Set is part of the symbol |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:604:| OD110 | <a id="od110"></a>Enums at the foreign boundary (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a value of enum type may cross an `extern fn` boundary, and if so in what representation. Bears on FFI-3, FFI-7. The current compiler: refused: `extern fn f(x: E)` with `enum Copy E { A, B }` is E0574 (a non-FFI-safe parameter); only scalars, raw pointers and `repr(C)` structs cross. The design record [LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md](../archive/design-records/LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md) says enums must not cross (§3.2); before it was archived it also said they cross as integers. Options: (a) refused, as in the current compiler; (b) a fieldless enum crosses as an integer of a stated width, which needs a stated discriminant for each variant. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | refused (E0574) | FFI-3, FFI-7 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refused |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:605:| OD111 | <a id="od111"></a>Misaligned fields of a packed struct (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a `#[repr(packed)]` struct may hold a field whose alignment exceeds the alignment of its offset, and whether such a field may be borrowed. Bears on FFI-4, FFI-8, REF-5. The current compiler: both accepted: `#[repr(packed)] struct P { v: u8, l: u32 }` places `l` at offset 1, and `&p.l` is accepted and reads the field correctly in the binary and in `--interp`. [LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md](../archive/design-records/LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md) §2 restricts a packed struct to fields whose alignment does not exceed their position, without saying what enforces it. Options: (a) refuse such a field; (b) accept it and refuse a reference to it; (c) accept both, as in the current compiler. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | both accepted on both consumers | FFI-4, FFI-8, REF-5 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept field, refuse a reference to it |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:606:| OD112 | <a id="od112"></a>Mutation through a shared reference (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a value reached through a shared reference `&T` may be changed, by a write through a raw pointer derived from the reference or by a cell type. Bears on REF-10, OWN-10, TRUST-2, TRUST-5. The current compiler: safe code changes a value only through a `mut` binding or a `&mut` reference, and there is no cell type; a trusted function may convert `r: &Int` to `*mut Int` (`r as *const Int as *mut Int`) and store through it: the binary does the store, and `--interp` stops ("deref-assign through immutable ref"). The archived [MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md](../archive/design-records/MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md) §12 said there is no `Cell`, `RefCell` or `UnsafeCell` equivalent and that mutation flows from `let mut` through `&mut`; the [overview](overview.md) lists no interior mutability in safe code, with a later `UnsafeCell<T>` gated by `Unsafe`. Options: (a) never: a write through a pointer derived from a `&T` is undefined behaviour, a premise trusted code keeps (TRUST-2); (b) as (a), with a cell type gated by `Unsafe` as the one exception; (c) a trusted write through such a pointer is allowed. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | safe code cannot; a trusted write through `r as *const T as *mut T` runs in the binary and stops `--interp` | REF-10, OWN-10, TRUST-2, TRUST-5 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Never; UB premise trusted code keeps |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:607:| OD113 | <a id="od113"></a>The ownership exceptions of trusted code (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether trusted code keeps its two exceptions to the ownership rules, destroying a value through a reference and releasing with `vec_free` a vector whose elements are not Copy. Bears on TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8. The current compiler: both are accepted in trusted code only (elsewhere E0290 under OWN-8 and E0287 under OWN-5): std's collections destroy an element through `(p as &T)` in drop glue after its owner was consumed, and a trusted function may free a buffer it has emptied. The archived [MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md](../archive/design-records/MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md) §11 said that `trusted` does not relax linearity, and TRUST-5's rationale says the same. Options: (a) keep both, each a named trusted operation with its premise (the owner was consumed; the vector is empty); (b) remove both, drop glue moving the element out of raw memory instead (TRUST-7); (c) keep only destruction through a reference; (d) keep only `vec_free` of an emptied vector. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | both accepted in trusted code only (E0290, E0287 elsewhere) | TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove both |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:608:| OD114 | <a id="od114"></a>How integer division rounds (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: How an integer division that does not abort rounds its quotient, and which sign a nonzero remainder takes. Bears on ARITH-3. The current compiler: the quotient is truncated toward zero and a nonzero remainder has the sign of the dividend, as C99 and LLVM's `sdiv` and `srem` give (`-7 / 2` is `-3`, `-7 % 2` is `-1`, `7 % -2` is `1`), in the binary, the interpreter and the constant folder (`IntArith.evalIntBinOp`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) states when division aborts (§5) but not how it rounds. Options: (a) truncation toward zero, as in the current compiler; (b) floored division, the remainder taking the divisor's sign; (c) Euclidean division, the remainder never negative. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the quotient is truncated toward zero and a remainder has the dividend's sign (C99) | ARITH-3 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Truncation toward zero |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:609:| OD115 | <a id="od115"></a>The body and totality of a specification function (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a `spec fn` may be declared without a body, and whether its body must be shown total. Bears on CON-3, CON-5, CON-1. The current compiler: both `spec fn big(x: Int) -> bool;` and `spec fn big(x: Int) -> bool = x > 10;` are accepted, and so is a recursive body that never terminates (`spec fn loopy(x: Int) -> bool = loopy(x + 1);`), with no check. [CONTRACTS_GUIDE.md](../verification/CONTRACTS_GUIDE.md) calls a `spec fn` "an erased mathematical specification, no body"; contracts.md's examples (CON-3) give it a body; [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Soundness Bridge) requires it to be total. Options: (a) a body is required and must be total (structural recursion or a checked measure); (b) a body is optional, a body-less one an uninterpreted function a proof must constrain, and a body must be total; (c) as in the current compiler, no totality check. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-115 | see OPEN-115 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Body required and total |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:610:| OD116 | <a id="od116"></a>A checked total function category (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether the language has a `total fn` category, a runtime function checked to terminate without trapping that contracts may then call. Bears on CON-1, CON-3, CON-5. The current compiler: there is no such category (`total fn f(..)` is a parse error, E0001); a contract clause may still call a capability-free runtime function until CON-3's judgment lands. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Design Principles) distinguishes `spec fn`, a checked `total fn`, ordinary and trusted functions, and admits a `total fn` into contracts once its obligations close. Options: (a) add the category; (b) no category: contracts call only `spec fn` (CON-3's full judgment). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-116 | see OPEN-116 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No category |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:611:| OD117 | <a id="od117"></a>`old`, quantifiers and implication in contracts (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether contract expressions gain `old(x)`, quantifiers and an implication or equivalence operator, and with what syntax. Bears on CON-2, CON-3, SYN-1. The current compiler: `#[ensures(result == old(x))]` compiles and runs, and `--report contracts` marks the clause `invalid_contract_expression` (unknown function `old`); `forall i: Int, ...` is a parse error (E0001); there is no implication operator. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Contract Expression V1) lists all three as deferred. Options: (a) add each, with a syntax decided per form; (b) keep them out, a claim needing one being a Lean theorem; (c) refuse `old(..)` now as an unknown name, deciding the rest later. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-117 | see OPEN-117 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No `old`, quantifiers or implication in contracts; state such properties as Lean theorems |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:612:| OD118 | <a id="od118"></a>A proven run-time violation in trusted code (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether an index the compiler proves out of bounds, or a division it proves to be by zero, is refused in `trusted` and `with(Unsafe)` code as in safe code. Bears on MEM-6, ARITH-3, TRUST-5, TRUST-12. The current compiler: `a[5]` on a `[Int; 3]`, or `x / 0`, is refused in safe code (E0900, `error[runtime-safety]`) and compiles in a `trusted fn` (the index also in a function declaring `Unsafe`), where it aborts at run time on both consumers. MEM-6 states the refusal with no exemption; [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) C7 records the exemption as intended. Options: (a) refuse everywhere, as MEM-6 reads; (b) keep the exemption and state it in MEM-6. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-118 | see OPEN-118 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse everywhere |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:613:| OD119 | <a id="od119"></a>Buffered output when the process aborts (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether output the program wrote before a trap or abort is flushed before the process ends. Bears on STMT-1, STMT-2, PASS-13, OPEN-101. The current compiler: print builtins write through C's buffered stdio, and `abort` does not flush it on glibc (a binary that prints `hello` and then traps prints nothing on Linux) while it does on Darwin; the interpreter models the same buffer and the same platform difference. STMT-2 says a failure ends the process at once and is silent on output already written, so the observable trace of a trapping program differs by platform. Recorded by [INTERPRETER_TRUST.md](../verification/INTERPRETER_TRUST.md) §2. Options: (a) everything written before the trap is output (flush on abort, or unbuffered output); (b) what was written is unspecified on a trap, and PASS-13 compares output only for exiting runs. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-119 | see OPEN-119 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Everything written before the trap is output |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:614:| OD120 | <a id="od120"></a>A vacuous contract (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a contract whose precondition is unsatisfiable is refused by the language or only reported, under a project policy. Bears on CON-1, CON-2. The current compiler: `#[requires(x > 5 && x < 3)]` compiles; a decided-unsatisfiable precondition makes the claim `vacuous`, never `proved`, and E0613 refuses it only under the `forbid-vacuous` policy (`Concrete.Policy`), not in a plain build. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Soundness Bridge) states the reporting rule. Options: (a) a language refusal in every build; (b) tooling only: always reported `vacuous`, refused under a policy, as in the current compiler. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-120 | see OPEN-120 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Tooling: reported `vacuous`, refused under policy |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:615:| OD121 | <a id="od121"></a>Projections from a base that is not a traceable place (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a field access, index or `*` whose base is not a traceable place (an `if` or `match` value, `**r`, a reference read out of storage) is refused, or accepted once the checker can trace it. Bears on REF-5, MEM-4, MEM-8, TYPE-7. The current compiler: refused (E0238, `untraceablePlace`), whether the projection reads a Copy value or forms a reference: `(if c { a } else { b }).f`, `(**r).f`, `*w.r`. No entry states the refusal: REF-5 forbids a branch value or a stored reference as a reference's root, and MEM-4, MEM-8 and TYPE-7 do not restrict a projection's base. [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) H28 calls it a deliberate restriction until R-0499's checker on Core. Options: (a) state the restriction as a rule (a projection's base is a place: a binding, its fields and elements, or `*` of a named reference); (b) accept such projections of owned values and Copy reads once the checker traces them, refusing only what REF-5 refuses. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-121 | see OPEN-121 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): State the restriction as a rule |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:616:| OD122 | <a id="od122"></a>A `repr(C)` struct returned from an extern function (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[repr(C)]` struct returned from an `extern fn` is returned as the platform's C calling convention returns it, at every size. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-11. The current compiler: a returned struct of at most 8 bytes comes back as one 64-bit integer, stored as 8 bytes into the struct's slot even when the struct is smaller (a 4-byte `in_addr` returned by `inet_makeaddr` reads back wrong natively); a larger one is returned as an LLVM aggregate, which matches C on x86-64 and AArch64 only for some layouts. [ABI.md](../platform/ABI.md) promised returns of at most 8 bytes and listed larger ones as not promised. Options: (a) every size, as C, classified by the `Layout` authority (R-0502); (b) at most 8 bytes (or 16), a larger return refused in an `extern fn` signature; (c) none, a returned struct going through an out-pointer the binding declares. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-122 | see OPEN-122 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Every size per C ABI via `Layout` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:617:| OD123 | <a id="od123"></a>`as` on a newtype outside its module (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether `v as N` and `n as T`, between a newtype `N` and its inner type `T`, are accepted outside the module that declares `N`, where TYPE-11 refuses `N(v)`. Bears on TYPE-11, TYPE-24, ARITH-5, OPEN-54. The current compiler: both accepted in any module, so `x as Id` builds a foreign newtype that `Id(x)` may not (E0296), and a validated wrapper's smart constructor can be bypassed. [VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md](../stdlib/VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md) §8 describes the cast rule as guarding the wrapper contract from an `as` bypass, which holds for casts to unrelated types only. Options: (a) accepted anywhere, as in the current compiler; (b) accepted only where `N(v)` and `.0` are (TYPE-11, with R-0500's follow-on for `.0`); (c) `as` never names a newtype, the conversions being `N(v)` and `.0`. Austral: no newtype (OPEN-54's comparison). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-123 | see OPEN-123 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `as` never names a newtype |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:618:| OD124 | <a id="od124"></a>Profiles in the specification (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether the profiles (the admission profiles Safe, Predictable and Proof, and the build profiles) belong to the language specification, in a chapter of their own, and whether a profile may change what an accepted program does. Bears on ARITH-9, OPEN-97, PASS-3, PASS-4. The current compiler: a profile only refuses or reports: `--check predictable` reports its five gates (no recursion, bounded loops, no allocation, no extern calls, no `File`, `Network` or `Process`), `[policy] predictable = true` in `Concrete.toml` turns a violation into E0610, and no profile changes the behaviour of a program it accepts; ARITH-9 states that for arithmetic. [PROFILES.md](../platform/PROFILES.md) says a profile never changes what a program means, while OPEN-97 asks whether a build profile may omit a run-time check. Options: (a) no profiles chapter: profiles are tooling, and the spec states only that no profile changes an accepted program's behaviour; (b) a profiles chapter whose entries state each profile's admission rules as refusals, with that same guarantee; (c) as (b), with build profiles allowed to differ as OPEN-97 decides. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-124 | see OPEN-124 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Profiles chapter of refusals; no behaviour change |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:619:| OD125 | <a id="od125"></a>Contracts at run time (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[requires]`, `#[ensures]` or loop `#[invariant]` that no proof discharges is checked at run time, trapping when it fails, or is only a claim. Bears on CON-1, CON-2, STMT-2. The current compiler: contracts are erased metadata: a call that violates `#[requires(x > 0)]` runs normally, in the binary and in `--interp`, and `assert` elaborates to proof evidence only (syntax chapter introduction). [ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md](../stdlib/ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md) lists contract-violation traps among the fatal failures. Options: (a) claims only, never checked at run time, as in the current compiler; (b) checked at run time and trapping unless discharged; (c) checked under a build profile only (OPEN-97, OPEN-124). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-125 | see OPEN-125 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Claims only |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:620:| OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:621:| OD127 | <a id="od127"></a>UTF-8 in every `String` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether every `String` value that safe code can reach holds valid UTF-8, as a language guarantee. Bears on TYPE-3, SYN-5, TRUST-2. The current compiler: literals are UTF-8 (SYN-5), but nothing keeps a `String` valid: `push_char` appends a byte unchecked, `Bytes::to_string` transfers bytes without validation, and std's `read_to_string` does not validate. [STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md](../stdlib/STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md) §1 states the invariant as a target, and [UNICODE_POLICY.md](../stdlib/UNICODE_POLICY.md) has a validating `Bytes::to_string` returning `Option`. Options: (a) a language guarantee, every safe constructor validating and an unchecked one being a trusted operation with that premise; (b) a std convention only, `String` being bytes to the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-127 | see OPEN-127 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Language guarantee |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:622:| OD128 | <a id="od128"></a>`->` between a match arm's pattern and body (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether a match arm may separate its pattern from its body with `->` as well as `=>`. Bears on SYN-1 and PAT-7. The current compiler: the parser accepts both (`parseMatchArms`; `2 -> { return 42; }` exits 42 on both consumers), and the grammar's `match_arm` has only `=>`. The syntax freeze review (2026-04, [SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW.md](../archive/design-records/SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW.md) §1.5) recommended `=>` as the canonical form with a warning for `->`, which also spells a return type. Options: (a) `=>` only, `->` refused; (b) both accepted; (c) both accepted, with a warning on `->`, as the freeze review recommended. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-128 | see OPEN-128 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `=>` only |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:623:| OD129 | <a id="od129"></a>Type arguments of an enum literal taken from its expected type (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether an enum literal or variant written without type arguments (`Option::Some { value: 42 }`, `Option::None`) takes them from the type its position expects, and from which positions. Bears on TYPE-7, TYPE-15 and OPEN-46, which asks the same of a generic call's type arguments and whose pick excludes the expected result type. The current compiler: the type arguments come from the expected type of a `return` (the function's result type), of an annotated `let` and of a call argument (the parameter's type); all three exit 42 on both consumers, and spec examples rely on it (PAT-1). The syntax freeze review (§1.3, kill-list items 1, 4 and 5) proposed this inference for enum literals and for constructor calls such as `Vec::new()`. Options: (a) explicit type arguments always; (b) from the expected type in the positions the current compiler uses; (c) the answer to OPEN-46, applied to enum literals. Austral: type parameters are inferred from the arguments, and a declaration's type can disambiguate. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-129 | see OPEN-129 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Same rule as OD46: type arguments from the arguments, otherwise explicit (`None::<i32>`) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:624:| OD130 | <a id="od130"></a>Unlabeled pattern binders: by position or by name (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether an unlabeled binder in a struct or variant pattern (`E::V { y, x }`) binds the field of its own name or the field at its position. Bears on PAT-1, PAT-3 and PAT-10. The current compiler disagrees with itself: with `enum E { V { x: Int, y: Int } }` and `E::V { x: 40, y: 2 }`, the arm `E::V { y, x } => y` gives 40 in `match` and in `if let` (binders zip the variant's fields in declaration order, `Concrete/Elab/Elab.lean` `bindings.zip ev.fields`), while the struct destructuring `let S { y, x } = s; y` gives 2 (by name); both consumers agree. PAT-1's `field: binder` form and the grammar's `binding_list` comment ("positional") point different ways. Options: (a) by name (field punning) everywhere; (b) by position everywhere; (c) refuse an unlabeled list whose order differs from the declaration. Austral: not applicable (no punning). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30; the variant-pattern disagreement may also be filed as a bug). | see OPEN-130 | see OPEN-130 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): By name everywhere |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:625:| OD131 | <a id="od131"></a>`union` declarations (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether the language has `union` declarations, and if so what reading a field other than the one last written means. Bears on SYN-1, TYPE-7, FFI-7 and FFI-8. The current compiler: `union U { a: Int, b: Uint }` parses (`Concrete/Frontend/Parser.lean`, as a struct whose fields share storage) and compiles; a value built as `U { a: 42 }` is linear, and `union` is neither a keyword nor in the grammar. No document specifies it. Options: (a) remove it; (b) keep it, for the foreign boundary only, with its layout and read rules specified. Austral: no unions. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-131 | see OPEN-131 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:626:| OD132 | <a id="od132"></a>Polymorphic recursion (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether a generic function that calls itself at a strictly larger type argument (`f::<Box<T>>` inside `f<T>`) is refused, and by which check. Bears on TYPE-13, PASS-7 and PASS-12. The current compiler: such a program is not refused; compilation does not terminate (more than 200 s and 2 GB on 2026-10-30, natively and with `--interp`), since monomorphization specializes without a bound on function instances (the bound `closeInstances` has covers only generic type definitions). [PASSES.md](../compiler/PASSES.md) §7 lists unbounded polymorphic recursion as Mono's error. Options: (a) refuse a call cycle whose type arguments grow, before Mono; (b) refuse at Mono when specialization exceeds a stated limit. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30; the non-termination may also be filed as a bug). | see OPEN-132 | see OPEN-132 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse growing call cycles before Mono |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:627:| OD133 | <a id="od133"></a>User functions named `print`, `println` or `append` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether a program may declare a function named `print`, `println` or `append`, which then replaces the builtin of that name in calls the program writes. Bears on MOD-3, MOD-8 and TYPE-10. The current compiler: accepted; with `fn print(x: Int) -> Int`, `print(42)` calls it (exit 42 on both consumers), because Elab desugars the variadic builtins only when no function of that name is visible, and MOD-3's reserved names do not include them. MOD-8 already binds the calls the compiler itself generates by identity. Options: (a) reserve the three names (MOD-3); (b) allow the replacement, as the current compiler does. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-133 | see OPEN-133 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Reserve the three names |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:629:| OD135 | <a id="od135"></a>A method's `Type_method` name in its module's namespace (added 2026-10-30, raised while reviewing the absorption of docs/compiler and docs/platform). Question: Whether an impl method `m` of a type `T` also occupies the name `T_m` in its module, so that a free function of that name is refused. Bears on MOD-3 and MOD-8. The current compiler: refused: `fn P_get(a: Int) -> Int` beside `impl P { fn get(&self) -> Int }` is E0299 under MOD-3@2 ("a function named 'P_get' is also declared in this module as an impl method"), on both consumers, because the method is entered under its mangled name. MOD-3's reserved names are the `__` prefix, `user_main` and the builtins, which do not include such names. Options: (a) keep it, listing `T_m` among the names MOD-3 reserves; (b) stop entering methods under `T_m`, so the free function is accepted. Austral: not applicable (no methods). Pick: none given (raised by the review of the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-135 | see OPEN-135 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Stop entering methods under `T_m` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:630:| OD136 | <a id="od136"></a>std's `process_exit` (added 2026-10-30, raised while reviewing the absorption of docs/compiler and docs/platform). Question: Whether std keeps `process_exit`, which ends the process through libc `exit` without running any deferred call or consuming the caller's owned values. Bears on STMT-7, OWN-2 and TRUST-4. The current compiler: `std.process.process_exit(code: i32) with(Process)` exists and calls libc `exit`; the language itself has no exit operation (STMT-7). The design record [MAIN_EXIT_MODEL.md](../platform/MAIN_EXIT_MODEL.md) calls an exit operation deliberately absent, since in a linear language it would bypass the proof that every owned value is consumed. Options: (a) keep it, stating that it skips cleanup and that `Process` is what grants it; (b) remove it, status flowing back through `main`'s return only. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the review of the absorption of docs/platform, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-136 | see OPEN-136 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:631:| OD137 | <a id="od137"></a>Character literals outside ASCII (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which byte a char literal denotes when its character is not ASCII. Bears on SYN-6, TYPE-20 and SYN-5. The current compiler: the literal silently takes the low byte of the character's code point: `'é'` (U+00E9) is 233, `'ā'` (U+0101) is 1 and `'€'` (U+20AC) is 172 as `u8`, on both consumers, and SYN-6 says only that a char literal is a token. Options: (a) refuse a char literal whose character is not ASCII, so every accepted literal is the one byte of its ASCII character; (b) accept U+0000 to U+00FF as that byte (Latin-1) and refuse anything above; (c) make `char` a Unicode scalar value, which changes TYPE-20's one-byte `char`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-137 | see OPEN-137 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse non-ASCII |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:632:| OD138 | <a id="od138"></a>Which positions give a literal its expected type (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which positions give an integer literal operand its expected type under ARITH-2@3 (and a float literal's, should OPEN-63 give floats the same rule). Bears on ARITH-2, TYPE-4 and TYPE-6. The current compiler: an unconstrained integer literal is typed `Int` and a float literal `Float64` (bug 327); with `x: u8`, a literal beside `x` takes `u8` as the other operand of `+`, `<` and `&` and as the count of `>>`: `x + 300`, `x < 300`, `x & 300` and `x >> 300` are each refused natively with E0227 (`integer literal 300 is out of range for type 'u8'`), and `300 as Int` is accepted, whether through `as` or the `Int` default the probe does not show. ARITH-2@3 refuses an integer literal with no expected type but does not list the positions that supply one. Options: (a) declared positions (an annotated binding, a parameter, a return, a field) and the other operand of a binary operator, as the probe shows the current compiler doing for `+`, `<`, `&` and a shift count (other operators were not probed); (b) declared positions only, so a literal beside a typed operand needs a typed binding; (c) option (a), plus the target type of an `as` for its literal operand. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-138 | see OPEN-138 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Declared positions + other operand of a binary op |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:633:| OD139 | <a id="od139"></a>A qualified call to a function that is not imported (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether `a::f()` may call a `pub` function of another module that the caller has not imported. Bears on MOD-2, SYN-10 and MOD-4. The current compiler: accepted: `mod a { pub fn answer() -> u8 { return 42; } }` and `a::answer()` in `main` exits 42 with no import, as MOD-4's and SYN-10's accepted examples also do, while MOD-2's sentence says another module uses a declaration only by importing it. Options: (a) a qualified path to a `pub` declaration is a use in its own right, and MOD-2 says so; (b) an import is required, and the unimported qualified call is refused. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-139 | see OPEN-139 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Import required |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:634:| OD140 | <a id="od140"></a>Implicitly dropped results of `free` and of a deferred call (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether the result of `free(h)` (the pointee, of type `T`) when `T` is Copy, and a deferred call's Copy result, may be dropped without `discard`, as exceptions to OWN-7@3 (OD29). Bears on OWN-7, OWN-17, MEM-5 and OWN-15. The current compiler: both accepted: `free(h);` on a `Heap<Int>` (whose result is the `Int` moved out of the cell: `let v: Int = free(h);` after `alloc(42)` gives 42 on both consumers) and `defer f();` with `fn f() -> u8` compile and run, while OWN-7@3 (decided OD29) drops a non-Unit result only through `discard(e)`. OWN-17 states the current compiler's drop of a deferred call's result, and MEM-5 and OWN-15 describe `free(h);` as a statement; neither is named as an exception in OWN-7. Options: (a) both are exceptions, stated in OWN-7; (b) neither is, so each needs `discard`; (c) `free` is an exception and a deferred call's result is not. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-140 | see OPEN-140 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Neither; each needs `discard`. Earlier clarification of 2026-11-02: `free` no longer exists (OD59); a deferred call's Copy result is dropped with `discard`, while a `Result` or `Option` cannot be discarded (OD62) and is handled by a named function that returns `Unit` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)). Revised 2026-11-02: `defer` takes a call whose result is `Unit` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)) **Canonical current deferred-result rule: the Unit-only audit rule below; the earlier discard-inside-defer fragment is superseded, and authority rule 9 refers to the Unit-only rule.** |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:636:| OD142 | <a id="od142"></a>`X.con` beside `X/mod.con` (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which file `mod X;` loads when both `X.con` and `X/mod.con` exist. Bears on MOD-4. The current compiler: `X.con` is loaded and `X/mod.con` is ignored without a diagnostic (with `X.con` returning 7 and `X/mod.con` 9, the program exits 7 on both consumers). MOD-4 says one module is loaded from either file. Options: (a) refuse the pair; (b) `X.con` takes precedence, as the current compiler does; (c) `X/mod.con` takes precedence. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-142 | see OPEN-142 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One module layout: `X.con`, children in directory `X/`; no `mod.con` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:637:| OD143 | <a id="od143"></a>What a contract may read (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which names each contract position (`requires`, `ensures`, `invariant`, `variant`) may read. Bears on CON-2, CON-3 and CON-4. The current compiler: CON-3 says a contract reads parameters and `result`, CON-4 lets contracts read `ghost let` bindings, and CON-2's accepted loop invariant reads the local `i`; the checker accepts all three. Options: (a) per position: `requires` the parameters, `ensures` the parameters, `result` and ghost bindings, and a loop's `invariant` and `variant` every binding in scope, with CON-3 amended; (b) every position reads every binding in scope at that point; (c) CON-3 as written, with loop contracts and ghost reads refused. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-143 | see OPEN-143 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): A contract reads exactly the names in scope at its position, plus `result` in `ensures` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:640:| OD146 | <a id="od146"></a>The result type of a test function (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether a `#[test]` function returns `i32` (STMT-10) or follows `main`'s decided `u8` or `Unit` (OD24). Bears on STMT-10 and STMT-7. The current compiler: `#[test] fn t() -> Int` is refused with E0319 (`#[test] function 't' must return i32`), and a test's result is its exit status under `concrete test`. Options: (a) keep `i32`; (b) `u8` or `Unit`, as `main` (STMT-7@3); (c) `Unit` only, a failing test aborting. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-146 | see OPEN-146 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `u8` or `Unit`, as `main`. Revised 2026-11-02: `u8` only, as `main` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-main)) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:641:| OD147 | <a id="od147"></a>`repr(C, packed)` together (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether `#[repr(C, packed)]` is accepted, and what layout it gives. Bears on FFI-4 and FFI-8. The current compiler: accepted: `#[repr(C, packed)] struct Copy P { a: u8, b: u32 }` has size 5, so packing overrides C alignment. FFI-4 refuses only `packed` with `align(N)`, and FFI-8 states C layout and packed layout separately. Options: (a) refuse the combination; (b) accept it as C field order with no padding and alignment 1, as the current compiler does, and FFI-8 says so. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-147 | see OPEN-147 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept: C order, no padding, align 1, stated in FFI-8 |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:642:| OD148 | <a id="od148"></a>Type parameters on a trait (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether a trait declaration may have type parameters, and if so what binds them at a bound and an impl. Bears on TRAIT-1, TRAIT-9 and SYN-1. The current compiler: `trait Tr<T> { fn get(&self, x: T) -> T; }` parses, but no bound or impl head can supply `T`, so `impl Tr for S { fn get(&self, x: u8) -> u8 ... }` is refused with E0582 and E0587 (`expected return type T, got u8`). OPEN-77 lists generic trait methods among the grammar's stale items; it covers only the productions, while this entry covers what the parameters mean and what binds them. Options: (a) refuse type parameters on a trait declaration; (b) allow them, with type arguments at bounds and impl heads (`impl Tr<u8> for S`). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-148 | see OPEN-148 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse type parameters on a trait |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:643:| OD149 | <a id="od149"></a>How `[e; N]` evaluates `e` (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: How many times an array repetition `[e; N]` evaluates `e`, and which `e` it accepts. Bears on STMT-4, OWN-15 and TYPE-7. The current compiler: `e` is evaluated once per element, in index order (`[bump(&mut n); 3]` gives `[1, 2, 3]` on both consumers), and whether a linear `e` is accepted was not shown (an array of a linear type that is never consumed is refused with E0208 either way). OWN-15 says only that an array literal moves each element in, and STMT-4 orders written elements. Options: (a) `e` is evaluated once and copied, so it must be Copy; (b) `e` is evaluated `N` times in index order, as the current compiler does; (c) `e` must be a constant expression. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-149 | see OPEN-149 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Evaluated once, must be Copy |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:645:| OD151 | <a id="od151"></a>Module paths of more than one level (added 2026-10-31, raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1). Question: Whether a qualified path may name a member of a module nested more than one level deep (`a::b::f()`). Bears on SYN-10, MOD-4 and OPEN-139. The current compiler: the parser's lowercase module-path branch accepts one qualification pair: `a::b::f()`, with `f` in `mod b` inside `mod a`, is a parse error on both consumers (E0001, `expected <, got ident(f)`), because after `a::b` it accepts only `::<` (explicit type arguments). SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1 gives `std::fs::read_to_string` as an example of module qualification, which this parser does not accept. Options: (a) one qualification pair, as in the current compiler, and SYN-10 says so; (b) any depth, every segment before the last naming a module (a repetition, which stays LL(1)). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-151 | see OPEN-151 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allow multi-level module paths `a::b::f` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:646:| OD152 | <a id="od152"></a>Whether a path's first letter decides its syntactic form (added 2026-10-31, raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1). Question: Whether the first letter of a path's first segment decides the syntactic form of `X::y`, or one path form leaves to name resolution whether `X` is a type or a module. Bears on SYN-10, SYN-1 and MOD-1. The current compiler: the parser chooses by the first letter of `X`: an uppercase `X` gives an associated-function call or an enum literal, and a lowercase `X` the flattened name `X_y`, which name resolution looks up as one identifier (bug 343); a module named with an uppercase letter goes through the associated-function form, and `M::f()` on `mod M` builds and runs on both consumers. SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1 presents the case split as how the parse stays LL(1). Options: (a) the first letter decides the syntactic form, as in the current compiler, and SYN-10 states it; (b) the parser reads `X::y` as one path form, which is also LL(1), and name resolution decides whether `X` is a type or a module. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-152 | see OPEN-152 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Resolve `X::y` by declaration, not by the first letter |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:647:| OD153 | <a id="od153"></a>Program functions named like a builtin (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether a program may declare a function whose name is a builtin's, other than `print`, `println` and `append` (OPEN-133) and the names MOD-3 reserves, and which function the program's calls of that name reach. Bears on MOD-3, MOD-1, MOD-8 and OPEN-133. The current compiler: a program function `string_length` is called by the program's calls on both consumers, and so are program functions named `string_len` and `String_len` (`string_len` returns 7 and `String_len` returns 9; a program that calls both exits 79 on both consumers), while `destroy`, `discard`, `abort`, `alloc`, `free`, `alloc_array`, `free_array` and `realloc_array` are reserved (E0578). The calls the compiler itself generates are bound by identity (MOD-8), so only the program's own calls change. Options: (a) reserve every remaining builtin's name, aliases included, and MOD-3 lists them (`print`, `println` and `append` stay with OPEN-133); (b) allow the declaration, and the program's own calls reach it, as the current compiler does, and MOD-3 says so; (c) allow the declaration, and a program reaches the builtin by a qualified name. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-153 | see OPEN-153 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse program functions named like builtins |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:649:| OD155 | <a id="od155"></a>`unwrap` beside TYPE-24's conversions (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether `unwrap` stays an intrinsic beside the conversions TYPE-24 lists (`n.0` and `n as T`), becomes one of those conversions, or is removed. Bears on TYPE-24, TYPE-11, TYPE-10 and OPEN-54. The current compiler: Check gives `unwrap(m)` the newtype's inner type (E0270 when the argument is not a newtype); `return unwrap(m);` exits 42 on both consumers, and `let x: Int = unwrap(m);` followed by `if x == 42` fails the native build at SSA verification (E0715, bug 345). TYPE-24 lists `n.0` and `n as T` and does not list `unwrap`. OPEN-54 decides this only if its answer removes newtypes; while newtypes stay, this question stays. Options: (a) keep `unwrap` as an intrinsic and leave it off TYPE-24's list, as the current compiler and TYPE-24 do; (b) `unwrap(n)` is a conversion, TYPE-24 lists it, and TYPE-11 restricts it as it restricts `n.0`; (c) remove `unwrap`, leaving `n.0` and `n as T`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-155 | see OPEN-155 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No builtin `unwrap`; use `match` |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:650:| OD156 | <a id="od156"></a>The capability of `get_args` (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Which capability the builtin `get_args` requires, or whether the builtin is removed. Bears on TRUST-4, STMT-7, TRUST-9 and OPEN-136. The current compiler: Check gives `get_args()` the result `HeapArray<String>` and requires `Process` (E0520 with `Env` only); the builtin has no implementation on either consumer (bug 347). STMT-7's rationale says command-line arguments come from `std.args` with `Env`, and OD8 gives `main` the root resource value (TRUST-9). Options: (a) `Env`, as `std.args` requires; (b) `Process`, as the current compiler's signature says; (c) another capability, or none; (d) remove the builtin, and command-line arguments come from `std.args`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-156 | see OPEN-156 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Superseded by the authority decision of 2026-11-01: `get_args` requires `with(Env)`, and `with(Alloc)` when it allocates. Revised 2026-11-02 by the owner's rule: it also takes the `Environment` grant, and an allocator when it allocates ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-filesystem)) |
legacy/docs/spec/decisions.md:651:| OD157 | <a id="od157"></a>How the type `Unit` is written (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether the type `Unit`, which STMT-7 names as a result type of `main` and which other rules and the builtins catalog also name, has a spelling in type positions, and which spelling. Bears on STMT-7, TYPE-4, SYN-1 and OPEN-66. The current compiler: a function with no result type returns no value, and `fn main() { return; }` exits 0; `fn main() -> Unit` and `let x: Unit = f();` are refused at resolve (E0108, unknown type 'Unit'); `fn main() -> ()` is a parse error (E0001, `expected type`). STMT-7@3 says `main`'s result type is `u8` or `Unit`. OPEN-66 concerns the unit value's literal; this entry concerns the type in type positions, and the two cross-reference each other. Options: (a) no spelling: the type is written only by omitting the result type, and STMT-7 says so; (b) `Unit` is a builtin type name, accepted wherever a type is; (c) `()` is the type's spelling; (d) both `Unit` and `()` are accepted. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-157 | see OPEN-157 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): The type is written `Unit`, its value `()` |
