legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:511:**ARITH-10@2.** `truncate::<T>(x)` keeps the low-order bits of `x`'s two's-complement representation, as many as `T`'s width, `reinterpret::<T>(x)` changes the signedness of the integer `x` at the same width and keeps its bit pattern, and `try_convert::<T>(x)` returns `Result<T, OutOfRange>`, with success containing the value of `T` that means the same number as `x` when there is one and failure otherwise (an integer out of range, a float that is not integral, out of range, NaN or infinite, or a value a target float does not represent exactly).
legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:521:**Rationale.** These are the conversions ARITH-5 sends every lossy `as` to, each with one stated
legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md:524:sign-changing `as` gives today. OD106 fixes `try_convert` as `Result<T, OutOfRange>`; OD99 fixes these builtin spellings and the float-rounding spellings `trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`. The float conversions are ARITH-11; implementation remains pending R-0500.
