legacy/docs/spec/MAINTAINERS.md:248:The gate runs on the built compiler and is registered as its own step in CI's `language-gates`
legacy/docs/spec/MAINTAINERS.md:262:| S4 examples | Every example behaves as §2.2 says, on both consumers, and an accepted example's extracted functions agree between the Lean model and the interpreter. |
legacy/docs/spec/MAINTAINERS.md:264:| S6 codes | Every code in `--report diagnostic-codes` has exactly one row in `codes.tsv`. The class is `rule` (named by an entry), `unmigrated:<chapter>`, `internal` (a compiler invariant failure, such as `ssa-verify` or `lower`), `tooling` (proof, policy and evidence tooling, not language) or `retired`. Class `rule` holds exactly for the codes some entry names. A code whose ledger stage is a language stage (`parse`, `resolve`, `check`, `elab`, `core-check`, `runtime-safety`) is a language refusal: its class is `rule` or `retired`, or `internal` with a reason in the fourth column saying why no program reaches it. For each code, the ledger's `rules` field equals the set of `<ID>@<version>` of the entries that name it. The spec owns that mapping, and the compiler's copy is checked against it. |
legacy/docs/spec/MAINTAINERS.md:266:| S8 tracking | Every tracking ID exists: an `R-` ID as a task row of ROADMAP.md or a row of the archived task disposition table (`docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/task_disposition.tsv`, which names where an archived task's obligations went), a `bug-` ID as a record in `docs/bugs/`, and an `OD` ID of an open entry in [docs/spec/decisions.md](decisions.md). |
legacy/docs/spec/MAINTAINERS.md:268:| S10 site | The site generator (§4) writes one page per chapter with every entry's anchor and the rule index, `scripts/gen/site_reference.py` writes the reference pages, and `zola build` succeeds on a scratch copy of `site/`. `check_site_generated.sh` keeps both sections generated: no tracked copy, every site build runs both generators first, and every site link resolves. Without `zola` the check fails, unless `SPEC_SKIP_SITE=1` is set outside CI, which counts it as SKIPPED. CI installs the pinned Zola that `book.yml` uses. |
legacy/docs/spec/MAINTAINERS.md:271:| S13 one specification | Lean is the only normative source as it lands; until then chapters state decided rules, with decisions.md governing disagreements. Every Markdown file under `docs/language` is listed in `unabsorbed.tsv` with the chapters that will absorb it; a file not listed is refused, a row whose file is gone is refused, and a row added relative to the base is refused, so the list only shrinks. `scripts/spec/one_spec.py` owns this source check and `check_docs_drift.sh` runs it on every push, apart from `check_spec.sh`, so that the gates which run the compiler do not depend on every `docs/language` file. Absorbing a document moves its rules into entries and its useful rationale under them, moves tracking material to ROADMAP or KNOWN_HOLES, and deletes the file and its row in one commit. Every file `absorb_inventory.tsv` names is a file in the tree with exactly one verdict row, and every unabsorbed `docs/language` file is in it. |
