legacy/docs/spec/SPEC.md:3880:#### ARITH-5. `as` converts without loss
legacy/docs/spec/SPEC.md:3882:**ARITH-5@4.** A numeric, `bool` or `char` conversion written with `as` is accepted only when every value of the source type converts to a value of the target type that means the same number, `bool` or byte, namely a type to itself, an integer to a wider integer of the same signedness, an unsigned integer to a wider signed one, `bool` to an integer, `char` to an unsigned integer of at least 8 bits or a signed one of at least 16, `u8` to `char`, an integer to a float that represents every value exactly, and `f32` to `f64` (the conversions outside this list that the current compiler still accepts, narrowing, a
legacy/docs/spec/SPEC.md:3892:- Obligation: one `IntArith` authority for conversions (R-0493); a lossless `as` needs no value-preservation obligation
legacy/docs/spec/SPEC.md:3895:**Rationale.** Decided 2026-10-21 (ARITHMETIC_POLICY §8, exit 2k): `as` is total and
legacy/docs/spec/SPEC.md:3896:obligation-free, and each lossy conversion names what it does. Today `as` refuses conversions to and from `bool` other than `bool` to an integer
legacy/docs/spec/SPEC.md:3904:Until then, in the current compiler, narrowing truncates silently (`300 as u8` is 44, `-1 as u32` is
legacy/docs/spec/SPEC.md:3905:4294967295, `3.9 as i32` is 3). Even a widening needs `as`: `want_u64(my_u8)` is E0220 (TYPE-4),
legacy/docs/spec/SPEC.md:3908:Decided by the owner on 2026-10-29 (OD11): `as` only when lossless by value domain, and every lossy conversion a named function.
