docs/decisions.md:1121:| OD6 | <a id="od6"></a>Traits are coherent: one impl per (trait, type), the orphan rule, an ambiguous method call must be qualified, resolution by typed identity | D7; implemented by bug 186's repair (TRAIT-1 – TRAIT-9) | bug 186's repair (exit 0b), so every deliverable; R-0548, R-0549; V5 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: traits are coherent: one impl per (trait, type), the orphan rule, an ambiguous method call must be qualified, resolution by typed identity. Completed 2026-11-02: qualified as `Trait::method(&x, ...)` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)) **Canonical current qualification rule: this OD6 as completed by the audit below; the audit supplies syntax, not a second rule.** |
docs/decisions.md:1136:| OD21 | <a id="od21"></a>Representation rights of a parent over its child module (added 2026-10-25, from bug 262): when a parent module imports its child's newtype or struct, every stage lets the parent construct the newtype and read and write the struct's private fields, because Check counts the children's declarations among the module's own for construction and field rights; Resolve also lets a parent name a child's newtype as a symbol without an import. Two options: enforce TYPE-11 as written (a newtype or private field is constructed or accessed only inside the module that declares it, so a child is another module), or amend TYPE-11 to grant a parent these rights over the declarations it imports from its children | the behaviour dates from construction rights slice 1 (2026-07-18); it contradicts TYPE-11@1's wording ("outside the module that declares it"); bug 262's record names `tests/programs/module_qualified_impl` as a program that applying TYPE-11 strictly would refuse; the wider impact is not yet measured (no `scripts/tools/rule_impact.sh` scan is recorded); bug 262's record (branch `wip/newtype-ctor-stage`) leaves the question to R-0011 and makes no recommendation | R-0011; the construction-rights fixtures (`module_qualified_impl` and bug 262's `error_262_*` and `regress_262_*` fixtures); bug 262's follow-up (whether Resolve binds a child's newtype in its parent) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: only the declaring module; a newtype is constructed, and a private field read or written, only inside the module that declares it, and a parent module gets no rights over its child (TYPE-11 enforced as written) |
docs/decisions.md:1158:| OD43 | <a id="od43"></a>How a type position resolves a name (added 2026-10-29, from the spec gap bug 229's ruling found). Options: (a) the current compiler's rules: type positions search the type namespace only, a user `enum Option` is always the builtin, and a parent sees its child's newtypes; (b) a user declaration shadows a builtin of the same name, with a warning; (c) a user type may not take a builtin type's name (refused). Recommendation: (c), which removes the shadowing question entirely; Austral reserves its builtin type names the same way Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (c) refused, for builtin-name collisions only, with the complete reserved set published (also for aliases and import renames); this answer would not retire OD43, whose type-versus-value lookup and child-module questions stay open in it. A user `struct Copy HeapArray` is treated as linear today | the current compiler follows (a) | MOD-1; bug 229's record | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse builtin-name collisions, publish reserved set. Completed 2026-11-02 by the owner: one namespace per module, and a child module's declarations only through `child::T` or an import ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-namespace)) |
docs/decisions.md:1159:| OD44 | <a id="od44"></a>Two packages of one program that define the same top-level module name (added 2026-10-29, from bug 074's ruling). Options: (a) refused when the project loads, naming both packages (the current compiler); (b) module paths are qualified by their package, so both coexist. Recommendation: (a) until packages depend on packages (R-0440), then revisit with (b) Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) refused at load, naming both packages, until packages depend on packages (R-0440) | the current compiler refuses (bug 074) | MOD-1, MOD-4; R-0440 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse at load, naming both, until R-0440 |
docs/decisions.md:1191:| OD76 | <a id="od76"></a>Unqualified struct patterns in `match` (added 2026-10-30, item G1-b of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether an unqualified struct pattern is allowed in `match` (`match s { S { a } => ... }`), given that only `let` destructures structs today (the `if let` form follows OD52). Options: (a) refused in `match`, PAT-1's wording fixed to say so; (b) allowed. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) refuse in `match`; fix PAT-1's wording. Austral: not checked | the parser refuses it in `match` and `if let` and accepts it in `let` | PAT-1, PAT-5, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse in `match`; fix PAT-1 |
docs/decisions.md:1192:| OD77 | <a id="od77"></a>Resync the grammar to the decided language (added 2026-10-30, item B of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `grammar/concrete.ebnf` is resynchronized for the items the language already decided: `E#V` becomes `E::V`, binding renaming, a function body with no trailing value and `;` for bodyless functions, no generic trait methods, assignment targets are places, unknown attribute names refused, and no `break <expr>`. Options: (a) approve the resync, as actual productions with a factored `place` prefix for assignment; (b) defer it, the divergences staying recorded in grammar/conformance_known.tsv (the decided language does not change either way). Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) approve, with actual productions, a `place` prefix and `break <expr>` removed. Austral: not applicable | the grammar is stale on these items (grammar/conformance_known.tsv) | SYN-1, PAT-1, STMT-6, STMT-8, OWN-8, FFI-6 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Approve resync with actual productions |
docs/decisions.md:1254:| OD139 | <a id="od139"></a>A qualified call to a function that is not imported (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether `a::f()` may call a `pub` function of another module that the caller has not imported. Bears on MOD-2, SYN-10 and MOD-4. The current compiler: accepted: `mod a { pub fn answer() -> u8 { return 42; } }` and `a::answer()` in `main` exits 42 with no import, as MOD-4's and SYN-10's accepted examples also do, while MOD-2's sentence says another module uses a declaration only by importing it. Options: (a) a qualified path to a `pub` declaration is a use in its own right, and MOD-2 says so; (b) an import is required, and the unimported qualified call is refused. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-139 | see OPEN-139 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Import required |
docs/decisions.md:1266:| OD151 | <a id="od151"></a>Module paths of more than one level (added 2026-10-31, raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1). Question: Whether a qualified path may name a member of a module nested more than one level deep (`a::b::f()`). Bears on SYN-10, MOD-4 and OPEN-139. The current compiler: the parser's lowercase module-path branch accepts one qualification pair: `a::b::f()`, with `f` in `mod b` inside `mod a`, is a parse error on both consumers (E0001, `expected <, got ident(f)`), because after `a::b` it accepts only `::<` (explicit type arguments). SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1 gives `std::fs::read_to_string` as an example of module qualification, which this parser does not accept. Options: (a) one qualification pair, as in the current compiler, and SYN-10 says so; (b) any depth, every segment before the last naming a module (a repetition, which stays LL(1)). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while absorbing SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW §1.1, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-151 | see OPEN-151 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allow multi-level module paths `a::b::f` |
docs/decisions.md:1268:| OD153 | <a id="od153"></a>Program functions named like a builtin (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether a program may declare a function whose name is a builtin's, other than `print`, `println` and `append` (OPEN-133) and the names MOD-3 reserves, and which function the program's calls of that name reach. Bears on MOD-3, MOD-1, MOD-8 and OPEN-133. The current compiler: a program function `string_length` is called by the program's calls on both consumers, and so are program functions named `string_len` and `String_len` (`string_len` returns 7 and `String_len` returns 9; a program that calls both exits 79 on both consumers), while `destroy`, `discard`, `abort`, `alloc`, `free`, `alloc_array`, `free_array` and `realloc_array` are reserved (E0578). The calls the compiler itself generates are bound by identity (MOD-8), so only the program's own calls change. Options: (a) reserve every remaining builtin's name, aliases included, and MOD-3 lists them (`print`, `println` and `append` stay with OPEN-133); (b) allow the declaration, and the program's own calls reach it, as the current compiler does, and MOD-3 says so; (c) allow the declaration, and a program reaches the builtin by a qualified name. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-153 | see OPEN-153 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse program functions named like builtins |
